"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that the required configuration exists **only for**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, among any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), none of the lines can be *sunny*.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting the points to be covered.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(\\displaystyle |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points (triangular number).\n\n2. **How many points can a single line contain?**  \n   *If a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)* (i.e. it is **not** sunny) then it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *If a line is sunny* then it can contain at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The bounds are proved by analysing the possible values of the sum \\(a+b\\) on a line and using the fact that the three forbidden directions partition the plane into strips of width\u202f1.\n\n3. **Upper bound for the total number of points covered by \\(n\\) lines.**  \n   Let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines among the \\(n\\) lines.  \n   Using the bounds from step\u202f2 we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\text{points covered}\\le s\\,(n-2)+(n-s)\\,n=n^{2}-2s .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Comparison with the required number of points.**  \n   From (1) and the count in step\u202f1 we need  \n   \\[\n   n^{2}-2s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad s\\le0 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(s=0\\); i.e. no sunny line can appear.\n\n5. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each line (2) contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to the corresponding constant.  Consequently the family (2) covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) positive integer solutions \\((a,b)\\). Summing over all admissible \\(s\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}t=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line in the plane.\n\n* (i) If \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) then  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n* (ii) If \\(\\ell\\) is sunny (i.e. not parallel to any of the three directions) then  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in one of the forms \\(y=c\\) (horizontal), \\(x=c\\) (vertical) or \\(x+y=c\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge1\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) are possible. The same bound holds for a vertical line.  \n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge2\\), the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(c\\le n+1\\); for a fixed \\(c\\) there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\). Hence in all three sub\u2011cases \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n\\).\n\nNow assume that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Write it as \\(y=mx+b\\) with slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nTake two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\).  Because the slope is not \\(-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\neq-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)\\neq0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the sums \\(a+b\\) of points lying on a sunny line are **pairwise distinct**.  \nSince the possible sums for points of \\(S_n\\) range from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\), at most one point of each sum can belong to \\(\\ell\\); consequently \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n\\).\n\nWe now show that the bound \\(n\\) cannot be attained.  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a sunny line contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Then it would have to contain a point for every admissible sum \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  Let \\((a_s,b_s)\\) be the unique point of sum \\(s\\) on \\(\\ell\\).  The sequence \\(\\{a_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) is strictly increasing (otherwise two points would have the same abscissa and the line would be vertical, contradicting sunniness).  Hence \\(a_{s+1}\\ge a_s+1\\).  Summing these inequalities from \\(s=2\\) to \\(n\\) yields  \n\n\\[\na_{n+1}\\ge a_2+(n-1).\n\\]\n\nBut \\(a_2\\ge1\\) and \\(a_{n+1}\\le n\\) (because \\(a_{n+1}+b_{n+1}=n+1\\) with \\(b_{n+1}\\ge1\\)).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nn\\ge a_{n+1}\\ge1+(n-1)=n,\n\\]\n\nso equality must hold throughout: \\(a_2=1\\) and \\(a_{s+1}=a_s+1\\) for every \\(s\\).  Consequently \\(a_s=s-1\\) and \\(b_s=1\\) for all \\(s\\).  All these points lie on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Hence a sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points.\n\nFinally we exclude the possibility of \\(n-1\\) points.  If a sunny line contained \\(n-1\\) points, it would miss exactly one sum, say \\(t\\).  The same monotonicity argument as above forces the abscissae to be consecutive integers, i.e. \\(a_s=s-1\\) for all \\(s\\neq t\\).  The missing sum forces a \u201cgap\u2019\u2019 in the sequence of abscissae, which would make the slope equal to \\(-1\\) (the line would join \\((t-2,2)\\) and \\((t,0)\\) after extending to integer coordinates), again contradicting sunniness.  Therefore a sunny line can contain **at most \\(n-2\\) points** of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound for a family of lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(s\\) are sunny. Then  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\ell\\cap S_n\\Bigr|\\le n^{2}-2s .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f2(i) each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); by Lemma\u202f2(ii) each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-2\\) points.  Adding these bounds for the \\(s\\) sunny and the \\(n-s\\) non\u2011sunny lines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}|\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|\n\\le s\\,(n-2)+(n-s)\\,n=n^{2}-2s .\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2011hand side is an upper bound for the cardinality of the union, (3) follows. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument  \n\nAssume that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nApplying Lemma\u202f3 with \\(s=k\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le n^{2}-2k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing Lemma\u202f1, \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  Substituting this into (4) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-2k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le 0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is a non\u2011negative integer, we must have \\(k=0\\).  Hence **no sunny line can appear** in any admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThey are pairwise distinct and each is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nFor a fixed integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(x+y=c\\) together with \\(x,y\\ge1\\) has exactly the points of \\(S