Thus the only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The set of points to be covered\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) positive\u2011integer solutions \\((a,b)\\). Summing over all admissible \\(s\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}t=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2. How many points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a single line?\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal*: \\(\\ell\\) has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) are possible.\n\n*Vertical*: analogous, using \\(x=c\\).\n\n*Parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)*: \\(\\ell\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge2\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\). For a fixed \\(c\\) there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions) then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nTake two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\).  \nBecause \\(m\\neq-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m\\neq-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)\\neq0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the sums \\(a+b\\) of points on a sunny line are **pairwise distinct**.  \nAll sums for points of \\(S_n\\) lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\); therefore at most one point of each sum can belong to \\(\\ell