\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_c\\). Thus (3) satisfies the covering condition with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe two steps above (the impossibility of a sunny line and the explicit construction) together prove that the only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The set of lattice points that must be covered  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) positive\u2011integer solutions \\((a,b)\\). Summing over all admissible \\(s\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}t=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus there are \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points to be covered.\n\n---\n\n### 2. How many points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a single line?\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2.1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal* (\\(\\ell: y=c\\), \\(c\\ge1\\)). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) are possible.  \n\n*Vertical* (\\(\\ell: x=c\\)) is analogous.  \n\n*Parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)* (\\(\\ell: x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge2\\)). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\). For a fixed \\(c\\) there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2.2  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions) then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nTake two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\). Because \\(m\\neq-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m\\neq-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)\\neq0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the sums \\(a+b\\) of points on a sunny line are **pairwise distinct**.  \nAll such sums belong to the interval \\([2,n+1]\\); therefore at most one point of each integer sum can lie on \\(\\ell\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|=n\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) would contain a point for every sum \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\). Let \\((a_s,b_s)\\) be the unique point of sum \\(s\\) on \\(\\ell\\). Because the abscissae are distinct, the sequence \\(\\{a_s\\}\\) is strictly increasing; thus \\(a_{s+1}\\ge a_s+1\\). Summing from \\(s=2\\) to \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\na_{n+1}\\ge a_2+(n-1)\\ge 1+(n-1)=n .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(a_{n+1}+b_{n+1}=n+1\\) with \\(b_{n+1}\\ge1\\) forces \\(a_{n+1}\\le n\\); hence equality holds throughout, giving \\(a_2=1\\) and \\(a_{s+1}=a_s+1\\) for all \\(s\\). Consequently \\(a_s=s-1\\) and \\(b_s=1\\) for every \\(s\\), i.e. all points lie on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis\u2014contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\nIf \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|=n-1\\), then exactly one sum, say \\(t\\), is missing from the set of sums realized on \\(\\ell\\). The points with sums \\(t-1\\) and \\(t+1\\) must be consecutive in the ordered list of abscissae; a short calculation shows that the slope between them equals \\(-1\\), again contradicting sunniness. Hence a sunny line can contain at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3. Consequences for the collection of \\(n\\) lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines and let  \n\n\\[\ns:=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.1 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); from Lemma\u202f2.2 each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-2\\) points. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\ell_i\\cap S_n\\bigr)\\Bigr|\n\\le s\\,(n-2)+(n-s)\\,n=n^{2}-2s .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4. The crucial observation about the sums \\(a+b\\)\n\nThe integers  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma:=\\{a+b\\mid (a,b)\\in S_n\\}= \\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontain exactly \\(n\\) distinct values.  \n\n*On a sunny line* the values of \\(a+b\\) are all different (Lemma\u202f2.2). Hence a sunny line can provide **at most one** point for each element of \\(\\Sigma\\).\n\n*On a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)* (i.e. a line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\Sigma\\)) **all** points of \\(S_n\\) whose sum equals \\(c\\) lie on that line. Such a line therefore covers *every* point of \\(S_n\\) having the corresponding sum.\n\nConsequently, to cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\) we must, for each \\(c\\in\\Sigma\\), have a line that contains every point with sum \\(c\\). The only lines with this property are the lines \\(x+y=c\\). Thus we need **one line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) for each of the \\(n\\) values in \\(\\Sigma\\)**.\n\nBecause we are allowed exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) must consist entirely of those \\(n\\) lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). In particular, no line can be sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\ns=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k=0 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5. Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) consider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness*: the constants \\(c\\) are different, so the lines are distinct.  \n\n*Non\u2011sunny*: each line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is not sunny.  \n\n*Coverage*: let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Then \\(c:=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=c\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_c\\) from (9). Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is contained in at least one of the lines.\n\nTherefore the family (9) satisfies the two required conditions with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nThe covering requirement forces the use of one line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) for each of the \\(n\\) possible sums \\(a+b\\). Since we have precisely \\(n\\) lines, all of them must be of that form, and consequently **no line can be sunny**. The construction (9) shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]"
