or each sum  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nFix a particular \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nThe set \\(\\Sigma_c\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points.  \n\n* Any line that is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contribute at most one of those points (Lemmas\u202f2.1 and\u202f2.2).  \n* Therefore, to cover the whole \\(\\Sigma_c\\) we need at least \\(c-1\\) lines that are not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), **unless** there is a line of the form \\(x+y=c\\), which already supplies all \\(c-1\\) points (Lemma\u202f2.3).\n\nSumming the required numbers over all values of \\(c\\) yields the inequality  \n\n\\[\nn\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) the right\u2011hand side exceeds \\(n\\), contradicting (2) unless **for every** \\(c\\) we actually have a line \\(x+y=c\\) in the family. Consequently the family must consist exactly of the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not sunny**.\n\nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4. Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe lines displayed in (3) are distinct, none of them is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\). Thus the family (3) meets the covering requirement with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nBoth the necessity (Section\u202f3) and the explicit construction (Section\u202f4) show that the only possible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]"
