\\ell_c\\) covers the missing point. Hence the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n   Because \\(s\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\), we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k=s\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   As shown in step 3, any admissible configuration must contain the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is not sunny. Therefore a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist; i.e. \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\le n-1\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
