{vertical}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;( \\text{the line }x+y=0).\n\\]\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nDenote by\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\nthe set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nThe cardinality of \\(T_{n}\\) is\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n} (n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\n*For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) consider the horizontal line\n\\[\n\\ell_{b}:\\; y=b .\n\\]\nAll \\(\\ell_{b}\\) are distinct, have slope \\(0\\) and therefore are not sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) then by definition \\(b\\le n\\) and consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{b}\\).  \nThus \\(\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}\\ell_{b}\\) contains every point of \\(T_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks on the general case\n\nThe lemma shows that the value \\(k=0\\) is always admissible.  \nTo obtain a configuration with \\(k>0\\) one must replace some of the horizontal lines by lines of other slopes while preserving the covering property and keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\nA few elementary observations are immediate:\n\n1. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Any two distinct sunny lines intersect in at most one point.  \n   Since each point of \\(T_{n}\\) may be used by several lines, this does not by itself limit \\(k\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   The three families of forbidden directions each consist of at most \\(n\\) distinct lines (horizontal, vertical, diagonal).  \n   If we use fewer than \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the remaining points must be covered by sunny lines, which are more restrictive because a sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three families.\n\n3. **Incidence counting attempt.**  \n   Suppose we have a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Let \\(I\\) be the total number of incidences between the lines and the points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   Each point of \\(T_{n}\\) must be incident to at least one line, so \\(I\\ge |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \n   On the other hand, a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (e.g., a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points).  \n   A sunny line can also contain at most \\(n\\) points, because any line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) meets each of the three forbidden families in at most one point, and there are at most \\(n\\) points of each family inside the triangle.  \n   Hence\n   \\[\n   I\\le k\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot n = n^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Combining the two inequalities yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n   which is always true and therefore does not restrict \\(k\\).\n\nThese elementary considerations do not suffice to decide which values \\(k>0\\) can actually be realized.  Constructing explicit examples for particular \\(k\\) (for instance by taking a few diagonal or vertical lines and filling the remaining points with suitably chosen sunny lines) is possible for small \\(n\\), but a general pattern has not been identified.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nWe have rigorously proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is admissible.  \nThe existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations with \\(k>0\\) remains an open problem within the scope of this answer."
