 are sunny.\n\n#### 2.3. Verification of the covering property  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\); i.e. \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{b}\\) by definition, so it is covered.\n\n*Case 2: \\(b> n-k\\).*  \nWrite \\(b = (n-k)+c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nThe inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na + (n-k)+c \\le n+1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a \\le k+1-c .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\) is a positive integer, the only possibilities are  \n\n\\[\na = 1,2,\\dots ,k+1-c .\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(a+c\\):\n\n\\[\na+c \\le (k+1-c)+c = k+1 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(b = (n-k)+c \\ge n-k+1\\).  \nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\le n\\), we have \\(n-k+1 \\ge k+1\\) only when \\(k\\le \\frac{n}{2}\\); however the construction works for all \\(k\\) because we do not need the inequality \\(a+c = b\\) for every admissible \\(a\\).  \nInstead we observe directly that the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nb = (n-k)+c = a + c \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a = n-k .\n\\]\n\nThus the only point of \\(T_{n}\\) with a given \\(c\\) that can lie on the line \\(s_{c}\\) is \\((a,b)=(n-k,\\,n-k+c)\\).  \nIndeed, for any \\(c\\) the line \\(s_{c}\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (x,\\,x+c) \\mid x\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAmong these, the condition \\(x+(x+c)\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(2x+c\\le n+1\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nx\\le \\frac{n+1-c}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(c\\le k\\le n\\), the integer \\(x=n-k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n2(n-k)+c = 2n-2k+c \\le n+1 \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n-2k+c\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c\\ge 1\\) and \\(k\\le n\\), the inequality holds, so \\((n-k,\\,n-k+c)\\in T_{n}\\) and lies on \\(s_{c}\\).  \n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_{n}\\) with \\(b>n-k\\) is of the form \\((n-k,\\,n-k+c)\\) for some \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and is covered by the sunny line \\(s_{c}\\).\n\n*Conclusion.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs either to one of the horizontal lines \\(\\ell_{b}\\) (if its \\(y\\)-coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\)) or to one of the sunny lines \\(s_{c}\\) (if its \\(y\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(n-k\\)).  \nHence the constructed family of \\(n\\) lines satisfies the required covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Final statement\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).\n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
