"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* Any line meets the set  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   in at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   Proof: projecting \\(T_{n}\\) onto the \\(x\\)-axis gives the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); a line can intersect each vertical line \\(x=m\\) (\\(1\\le m\\le n\\)) in at most one point, hence at most \\(n\\) points overall.\n\n   Since \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), the \\(n\\) lines together must provide at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) incidences with the points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each would contribute at most \\(n\\) incidences, giving at most \\(n^{2}\\) incidences, which is insufficient because \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\) is **false**; the correct comparison is the opposite: \\(n^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 1\\).  \n   Hence the incidence count does **not** forbid all lines from being sunny.  \n   The real restriction comes from the three forbidden directions. A sunny line is never horizontal, never vertical and never of slope \\(-1\\). Consequently a sunny line can intersect each of the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) in at most one point. Because the set \\(T_{n}\\) contains **exactly** one point on each of those horizontal lines, a sunny line can cover **at most one** point of \\(T_{n}\\) on each horizontal level. Therefore, to cover all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points we need at least one line that is not sunny (otherwise we would have at most \\(n\\) points covered, one per horizontal level). Hence the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Take the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{b}:\\; y=b ,\\qquad b=1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   They are not sunny (slope \\(0\\)).  \n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{c}:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(s_{c}\\) is \\(1\\); this is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so each \\(s_{c}\\) is sunny.  \n\n   The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   *Verification of the covering property.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\); i.e. \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   - **Case 1.** \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \n     Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{b}\\).\n\n   - **Case 2.** \\(b> n-k\\).  \n     Write \\(b=(n-k)+c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     a\\le n+1-b = n+1-(n-k)-c = k+1-c .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(c\\ge 1\\), inequality (1) forces \\(a\\le k\\).  \n     Moreover, (1) gives \\(a\\le k+1-c\\), i.e. \\(a+c\\le k+1\\).  \n     Adding \\(c\\) to both sides of \\(a\\le k+1-c\\) yields  \n\n     \\[\n     a+c\\le k+1 .\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     On the other hand, from the definition of \\(b\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     b = (n-k)+c = a + (n-k-a) + c .\n     \\]\n\n     Using \\(a\\le k+1-c\\) we get \\(n-k-a \\ge n-2k-1+c\\). Since \\(k\\le n\\), the right\u2011hand side is non\u2011negative, and consequently  \n\n     \\[\n     b = a + c + (n-k-a) = a + c + \\underbrace{(n-k-a)}_{\\ge 0}.\n     \\]\n\n     The term \\((n-k-a)\\) is an integer, so \\(b\\) can be written as \\(a + c\\) **plus a non\u2011negative integer**.  \n     Howeve