the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S|=1+2+\\dots +n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nFix a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S\\).  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c .\n\\]\n\nBecause the points of \\(S\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates ranging from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\), each \\(V_c\\) contains exactly one point of \\(S\\); denote it by \\(P_c\\).\n\nA sunny line can intersect a given vertical line in **at most one** point (otherwise the two intersection points would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical, which is forbidden). Consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of the points \\(\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_n\\}\\).\n\nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, then at most \\(n-1\\) of the points \\(P_c\\) could be covered by sunny lines, leaving at least one \\(P_{c_0}\\) uncovered by any sunny line. That remaining point must then be covered by a non‑sunny line. A non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n* It cannot be vertical, because the vertical line \\(V_{c_0}\\) already contains \\(P_{c_0}\\) and would coincide with a line already counted among the \\(n\\) distinct lines, contradicting distinctness.\n* It cannot be of slope \\(-1\\), because any line of slope \\(-1\\) meets each vertical line in a distinct point, so it would already have been counted as a sunny line (its slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, but such a line is not sunny; however, a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point of each vertical line, so it could cover at most one of the \\(P_c\\), contradicting the fact that all \\(P_c\\) except possibly one are already covered by sunny lines).\n* Hence the only possibility is that the line is horizontal, i.e. \\(y=b\\) for some integer \\(b\\).  \n  A horizontal line contains all points of \\(S\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\). In particular it contains the point \\((1,b)\\). Since the vertical line \\(V_1\\) already supplies the point \\((1,b)\\) to the covering, the horizontal line would duplicate a point already covered, but this does not resolve the uncovered point \\(P_{c_0}\\) unless \\(c_0=1\\). In the generic case \\(c_0\\neq 1\\) we obtain a contradiction.\n\nA more systematic counting argument avoids the case analysis.  \nEach non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (the whole set of points with a fixed \\(y\\)-coordinate, or the whole set with a fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate, or the diagonal \\(a+b=\\text{const}\\)).  \nIf there were \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, the total number of points that can be covered would be at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\cdot 1 + 1\\cdot n = 2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBut by (1) we have \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge 2n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), and equality holds only for \\(n=3\\). For \\(n=3\\) a direct inspection shows that a configuration with \\(k=2\\) sunny lines is impossible (the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines and one non‑sunny line). Hence for all \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), hence lies on \\(H_b\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore none is sunny.  \n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) consider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd_i:\\; x+y=n+1-i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(d_i\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(n+1-i\\); there are precisely \\(i\\) such points, namely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,n-i),\\;(2,n-i-1),\\dots ,(i,n-2i+1)\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(d_i\\) is \\(-1\\), which is **not** allowed for a sunny line.  \nTo obtain a sunny line we perturb \\(d_i\\) by an infinitesimal irrational amount.  \nChoose distinct irrational numbers \\(\\varepsilon_1,\\dots ,\\varepsilon_k\\) with \\(|\\varepsilon_i|<\\frac{1}{10n}\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\tilde\\ell_i:\\; y = (-1+\\varepsilon_i)x + (n+1-i).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\varepsilon_i\\neq 0\\) and \\(\\varepsilon_i\\neq -1\\), the slope of \\(\\tilde\\ell_i\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(\\tilde\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n\n*Why the integer points (4) remain on \\(\\tilde\\ell_i\\).*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfying \\(a+b=n+1-i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = (-1+\\varepsilon_i)a + (n+1-i)\n      \\iff \\varepsilon_i a = 0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\varepsilon_i\\neq 0\\) and \\(a\\) is a positive integer, the equality cannot hold.  \nHence the perturbed line \\(\\tilde\\ell_i\\) **does not** pass through the points of (4).  \nTo keep those diagonal points covered we *do not* perturb the line; instead we keep the original diagonal line \\(d_i\\) **as is** and note that a line of slope \\(-1\\) is *allowed* as a non‑sunny line.  \nThus we modify the construction: we keep the \\(k\\) diagonal lines \\(d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\) (which are non‑sunny) and replace **\\(k\\) of the horizontal lines** by **\\(k\\) sunny lines of a different slope**, for example lines of slope \\(1\\) that pass through the same diagonal points.\n\nA simpler and fully rigorous construction is the following.\n\n*Construction.*  \n\n1. For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y = x + i .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\), hence it is sunny.  \n   The line \\(s_i\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) of \\(S\\) with \\(b-a=i\\).  \n   Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n\\), the possible values of \\(a\\) are \\(1\\le a\\le n-i\\); consequently \\(s_i\\) covers the set  \n\n   \\[\n   D_i=\\{(a,a+i)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-i\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n2. Remove from the family of horizontal lines exactly those horizontal lines that would duplicate points already covered by the sunny lines.  \n   The horizontal lines that are still needed are  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\; y=j\\qquad\\text{for }j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(n-k\\) of them, all non‑sunny.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S\\).  \nIf \\(b-a\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D_{b-a}\\) and the point lies on the sunny line \\(s_{b-a}\\).  \nOtherwise \\(b-a\\ge k+1\\). Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) we have \\(b\\ge a+k+1\\ge k+1\\); thus \\(b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n\nHence every point of \\(S\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\) together with the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\).\n\n*Distinctness of the lines.*  \nThe slopes of the \\(k\\) sunny lines are all equal to \\(1\\), but their intercepts differ, so the lines are distinct.  \nThe horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\) and distinct intercepts, so they are also distinct.  \nNo sunny line coincides with a horizontal line because their slopes differ.  \n\nThus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (2) we know that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
