pe \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nIn any case a non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (the horizontal and vertical cases are obvious; a line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with exactly one diagonal, whose size is at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\le n\\)).\n\nNow suppose that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S\\) there are \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Each sunny line* can contribute at most one point from each diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points in total.  \nHence the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\,(n-1)\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Each non‑sunny line* covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); therefore the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points.\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le k\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n = n^{2}-k. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\ninequality (1) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n      =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInequality (2) is weaker than the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\); we need a sharper restriction.\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that **only one** of the \\(n\\) lines is non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).  \nThe single non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nAll remaining points must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, each of which can contribute at most one point per diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points in total.  \nThus the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)=2n-1.\n\\]\n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-1\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3.\n\\]\n\nHence at least **two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, which means  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y = x + i . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(s_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore each \\(s_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on \\(s_i\\) exactly when \\(b-a=i\\).  \nTogether with the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) this gives  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-i}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the set of points of \\(S\\) covered by \\(s_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a,a+i)\\mid 1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-i}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\bigr\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) take the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nh_j:\\; y=j . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they are distinct because the ordinates differ.\n\nAltogether we have \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n**Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on at least one of the lines.**\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(b-a=i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nThen (5) holds, so \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(s_i\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b-a\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nThen either \\(b\\le k\\) or \\(b\\ge k+1\\).\n\n*Subcase 2a.* \\(b\\le k\\).  \nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\) we have \\(b\\le n-k\\); consequently the horizontal line \\(h_b\\) (which is among the lines (7)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Subcase 2b.* \\(b\\ge k+1\\).  \nSince every point of \\(S\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n\\) and \\(b\\ge k+1\\), we have \\(b\\le n-k\\) as well (the inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) guarantees \\(k+1\\le n-k\\)). Hence the horizontal line \\(h_b\\) is again one of the lines (7) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) is covered by at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(s_i\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny horizontal lines, satisfying both requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nInequality (3) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nThe explicit construction above demonstrates that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible non‑negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
