ry point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them.  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most one point from each diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\) in total. Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\,(n-1)\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (or a vertical line \\(x=c\\)) intersects the triangular region \\(\\{a\\ge1,b\\ge1,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with exactly one diagonal \\(D_{t}\\); the size of any diagonal is at most \\(n\\). Consequently each non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\). Therefore the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nAdding the two estimates we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S|\\;\\le\\;k\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n = n^{2}-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince the set \\(S\\) consists of all integer points in a right‑angled triangle of side length \\(n\\), its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nInsert this into (1):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n      =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\), the right‑hand side is at least \\(2\\); more importantly, the inequality can be rewritten as\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\nn^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}=n-2+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\ge n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final statement\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\), if there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines among them necessarily fulfills  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\nThe converse – showing that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration actually exists – has not been established in this answer.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
