            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) put  \n\n\\[\nD_{t}= \\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=t\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_{t}\\) are pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\n|D_{t}|=t-1\\qquad(2\\le t\\le n+1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n\nA line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_{t}\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  The diagonal \\(D_{t}\\) is the set of solutions of the linear equation \\(x+y=t\\).  \nA sunny line has an equation \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nThe system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx+y=t,\\\\\ny=mx+c\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nhas a unique solution because the two equations are not multiples of each other; consequently at most one point of \\(D_{t}\\) lies on the line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) such that every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them.  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line can contain at most one point from each diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\,(n-1)\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (or a vertical line \\(x=c\\)) intersects the triangular region \\(\\{a\\ge1,b\\ge1,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with exactly one diagonal \\(D_{t}\\); by (2) each diagonal contains at most \\(n\\) points.  \nThus each non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\), and the \\(n-k\\) such lines together cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points.\n\nAdding the two estimates we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le k\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n=n^{2}-k . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) in (4) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n**(i) A configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.**  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{1-i}\\,x+\\frac{i}{1-i}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i+1)\\); consequently it meets diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\) at \\((i,1)\\) and diagonal \\(D_{i+2}\\) at \\((1,i+1)\\).  \nIts slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{1-i}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe set \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2}\\}\\) covers all points of the diagonals \\(D_{3},D_{4},\\dots ,D_{n}\\).  \nThe two remaining diagonals are  \n\n\\[\nD_{2}=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad\nD_{n+1}=\\{(n,1),(1,n)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAdd the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nThe line \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and all points \\((2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) of \\(D_{n+1}\\); the line \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \nConsequently the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{\\max }=\\bigl\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2},\\; y=1,\\; x=1\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncover every point of \\(S\\) and contain exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n**(ii) Obtaining fewer sunny lines.**  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nStart from \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\max }\\) and replace arbitrarily \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda:\\; x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.  \nThe line \\(\\lambda\\) coincides with the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\); all points of this diagonal are already covered by the two lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\), so the replacement does not destroy the covering property.  \n\nAfter performing exactly \\(n-2-k\\) replacements we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(S\\) and now contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny).  \n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) such a configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final Result  \n\nCombining the upper bound (5) with the explicit constructions, we conclude that the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
