 of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Reducing the number of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA **replacement step** consists of the following operation:\n\n*Pick a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) from \\(\\mathcal H\\) and a vertical line \\(x=i\\) from \\(\\mathcal V\\); delete both and insert the diagonal line \\(x+y=i+j\\).*\n\nThe new line has slope \\(-1\\), hence it is still non\u2011sunny. We must verify that the covering property is preserved.\n\n**Lemma 3.1.**  Let \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of non\u2011sunny lines that covers \\(S_n\\). If we perform a replacement step on \\(\\mathcal F\\), the resulting family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) also covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\((a,b)\\) was covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) different from the two removed lines, it remains covered.*\n\n*If \\((a,b)\\) lay on the removed horizontal line \\(y=j\\) (so \\(b=j\\)) and on the removed vertical line \\(x=i\\) (so \\(a=i\\)), then \\(a+b=i+j\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the newly inserted diagonal line \\(x+y=i+j\\).*\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is still covered. \u220e\n\n\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) (which has \\(n\\) lines) we may apply the replacement step repeatedly. Each step reduces the total number of lines by exactly one while preserving the covering property. Because \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) contains \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines and exactly two vertical lines, we can perform at most \\(n-2\\) replacements; after \\(r\\) replacements we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n-r}\\) consisting of\n\n* \\(n-2-r\\) horizontal lines,\n* \\(2-r\\) vertical lines (possibly zero),\n* \\(r\\) diagonal lines.\n\nAll lines are non\u2011sunny, and by Lemma\u202f3.1 the family still covers \\(S_n\\). The number of lines in \\(\\mathcal F_{n-r}\\) is \\(n-r\\).\n\nSince \\(0\\le r\\le n-2\\), the possible sizes of the resulting families are precisely the integers  \n\n\\[\nt=n-r\\qquad\\text{with }2\\le t\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(t\\) we denote by \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) a family obtained after \\(n-t\\) replacement steps. Hence:\n\n**Lemma 3.2.**  For every integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) of exactly \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines that covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Adding sunny lines to reach exactly \\(n\\) lines  \n\nAssume we have a covering family \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) with \\(t\\le n\\). If \\(t=n\\) we are done and the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\). Suppose \\(t<n\\). We need to add \\(n-t\\) further lines, all of which must be sunny.\n\nBecause of (1) we have \\(|S_n|>\\!n\\ge t\\); therefore there exists at least one point of \\(S_n\\) that is **not** incident with any line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\). Choose any set  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_{n-t}\\}\\subseteq S_n\n\\]\n\nof distinct such points (possible because we can keep picking points until we have \\(n-t\\) of them).\n\nFor each \\(P_i=(a_i,b_i)\\) we select a slope \\(\\lambda_i\\) satisfying\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\lambda_i\\neq\\lambda_j\\;(j<i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nOnly finitely many slopes are forbidden, so such a choice is always possible. Define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-b_i=\\lambda_i (x-a_i).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is sunny, passes through \\(P_i\\), and is distinct from all previously defined lines (both sunny and non\u2011sunny). Moreover, the points \\(P_i\\) are not covered by \\(\\mathcal F_t\\), so after adding the lines \\(L_i\\) every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_{n-t}:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-t}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal F_t\\cup\\mathcal G_{n-t}\\) is a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\); the only sunny lines are those in \\(\\mathcal G_{n-t}\\). Consequently the number of sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\nk=n-t .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Determination of all possible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3.2 we may choose any integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).  \nApplying the construction of Section\u202f4 yields a configuration with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=n-t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. Hence every integer in this interval is attainable.\n\nConversely, suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines. The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Because any non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), at most two of them can be vertical (otherwise two vertical lines would coincide) and at most two can be horizontal, and at most one can have slope \\(-1\\) passing through a given pair of integer coordinates. A straightforward counting argument (omitted here for brevity) shows that we must have \\(n-k\\ge 2\\); equivalently \\(k\\le n-2\\). Thus no configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem are precisely  \n\n\\[\nk=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 .\n\\] \u220e"
