nes are sunny, distinct, and together with \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) give a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\). The number of sunny lines is \\(k=n-t\\).\n\n4. **Upper bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   * Any sunny line has slope not in \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n   * At most two non‑sunny lines can be vertical, at most two can be horizontal, and at most one can have slope \\(-1\\) without coinciding with another line. Hence any covering family must contain at least two non‑sunny lines. Consequently \\(n-k\\ge 2\\), i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n   * Steps 2–3 show that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n   * Step 4 shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \n   Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set that must be covered  \n\nFix an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor later use we note the cardinality\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\nOur goal is to produce, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(S_n\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A basic covering by non‑sunny lines  \n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n:=\\bigl\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-2\\bigr\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{x=1,\\;x=2\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) consists of \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines and two vertical lines; clearly \\(|\\mathcal C_n|=n\\).\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \n\\(\\mathcal C_n\\) covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal C_n\\).\n\n* If \\