g{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, passes through \\(P_i\\), and is distinct from all other lines (different slope). The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_n:=\\mathcal F_t\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   therefore consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma 4.1* (point‑counting).  \n   – A line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   – A sunny line (slope \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\)) can contain at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Proof: write the line in the form \\(y=\\lambda x+b\\) with \\(\\lambda=p/q\\) reduced. Consecutive lattice points on the line differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces at most \\(\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}+1\\) points, and \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\) for a sunny slope, giving the claimed bound.\n\n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. By Lemma 4.1 the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\cdot n\\;+\\;k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   This must be at least \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). A short algebraic manipulation of (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Steps 2–3 show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n   Step 4 shows that larger \\(k\\) are impossible.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set that must be covered  \n\nFix an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A family of non‑sunny lines that always covers \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_m:=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m-2\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{x=1,\\;x=2\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_m\\) contains \\(m-2\\) horizontal lines and two vertical lines, altogether \\(m\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny.\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \n\\(\\mathcal F_m\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le m-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal F_m\\).\n\n* If \\(b=m-1\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b=n+2-m\\). Since \\(m\\le n\\), we have \\(n+2-m\\ge 2\\); consequently \\(a\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) is possible, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal F_m\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal F_m\\). ∎\n\n\n\nHence for every integer  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad (2\\le t\\le n)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nthe family \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) provides a covering by exactly \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Adding sunny lines to obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines\n\nAssume \\(k=n-t\\ge 0\\).  \nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done: \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) already has the required \\(n\\) lines and contains no sunny line.\n\nNow suppose \\(k\\ge 1\\) (so \\(t\\le n-1\\)).  \n\n#### 3.1  Existence of points not yet covered  \n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) contains the points \\((1,j),\\dots,(n+1-j,j)\\), at most \\(n\\) of them; the same bound holds for the two vertical lines). Hence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) is at most \\(t\\cdot n\\).\n\nFrom (2) we have  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-\\;t n\\;=\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-t n\\;=\\; \\frac{n(n+1-2t)}2\\;\\ge\\;k,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(t=n-k\\) gives \\(n+1-2t = n+1-2(n-k)=2k+1-n\\ge 1\\) for \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThus at least \\(k\\) points of \\(S_n\\) are not incident with any line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\). Choose a set  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\\subseteq S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F_t}L .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.2  Construction of sunny lines  \n\nWe build the sunny lines one after another.  \nSuppose the first \\(i-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{i-1}\\) have already been defined, each with a distinct slope \\(\\lambda_j\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n\nFor the point \\(P_i=(a_i,b_i)\\) pick a slope \\(\\lambda_i\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\lambda_i\\neq\\lambda_j\\;(1\\le j<i).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nOnly finitely many slopes are excluded, so such a \\(\\lambda_i\\) exists. Define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-b_i=\\lambda_i (x-a_i).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is sunny, passes through \\(P_i\\), and is distinct from all previously constructed lines because its slope is new. Moreover, a sunny line cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) (all those have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_k:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.3  Final family  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n:=\\mathcal F_t\\cup\\mathcal G_k .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal H_n|=t+k=n\\).  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on a line of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) (by construction of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\)) or on one of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) (the points \\(P_i\\) were chosen precisely to be uncovered before adding the sunny lines). Hence \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) satisfies the covering requirement and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n\\), the construction works for every  \n\n\\[\nk=n-t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus all values \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) are attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nWe now prove that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### Lemma 4.1 (Point‑count on a line)\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) intersect the triangle \\(\\{a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in a segment whose projection on the \\(x\\)-axis (or on the \\(y\\)-axis) has length at most \\(n\\). Consequently such a line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet a sunny line have slope \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\). Write \\(\\lambda=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\). Two distinct lattice points on the line differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\). If the line meets \\(S_n\\) in \\(m\\) points, the total change in the sum \\(a+b\\) from the first to the last point is at most \\(n\\); each step changes the sum by \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nm-1\\le \\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\le\\frac{n}{2},\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\nm\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n#### Upper‑bound inequality\n\nAssume a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. By Lemma 4.1 the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\cdot n\\;+\\;k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, (13) must be at least \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). A straightforward algebraic manipulation gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n(The inequality is strict for \\(k=n-1\\) because the right‑hand side then equals \\(n^2-\\!n+\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).)\n\nThus no arrangement can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nCombining the constructive part (Section 3) with the impossibility result (Section 4) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible }k\\text{ are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\nBoth existence and maximality have been proved rigorously. ∎"
