            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set that must be covered  \n\nFix an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A family of non‑sunny lines that always covers \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_t:=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le t-2\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{x=1,\\;x=2\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) contains \\(t-2\\) horizontal lines and two vertical lines, altogether \\(t\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny.\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \n\\(\\mathcal N_t\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(b\\le t-2\\).  \nThen the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal N_t\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b=t-1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-b=n+2-t\\). Since \\(t\\le n\\), we have \\(n+2-t\\ge 2\\); consequently \\(a\\) can be either \\(1\\) or \\(2\\