a_j\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n\nFor the point \\(P_i=(a_i,b_i)\\) pick a slope \\(\\lambda_i\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\lambda_i\\neq\\lambda_j\\;(1\\le j<i).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nOnly finitely many slopes are excluded, so such a \\(\\lambda_i\\) exists. Define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-b_i=\\lambda_i (x-a_i).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is sunny, passes through \\(P_i\\), and is distinct from all previously constructed lines because its slope is new. Moreover, a sunny line cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) (all those have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_k:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.3  Final family  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n:=\\mathcal N_t\\cup\\mathcal S_k .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal H_n|=t+k=n\\).  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on a line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) (by Lemma 2.1) or on one of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) (the points \\(P_i\\) were chosen precisely to be uncovered before adding the sunny lines). Hence \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) satisfies the covering requirement and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\n#### Lemma 4.1 (Point‑count on a line)\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) intersects the triangle \\(\\{a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in a segment whose projection on the \\(x\\)-axis (or on the \\(y\\)-axis) has length at most \\(n\\). Consequently such a line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet a sunny line have slope \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\). Write \\(\\lambda=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\). Two distinct lattice points on the line differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\). If the line meets \\(S_n\\) in \\(m\\) points, the total change in the sum \\(a+b\\) from the first to the last point is at most \\(n\\); each step changes the sum by \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nm-1\\le\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\le\\frac{n}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\nm\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n#### Upper‑bound inequality\n\nAssume a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. By Lemma 4.1 the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\cdot n\\;+\\;k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, (12) must be at least \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). A straightforward algebraic manipulation gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n(The inequality is strict for \\(k=n-1\\) because the right‑hand side then equals \\(n^2-n+\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).)\n\nThus no arrangement can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nCombining the constructive part (Section 3) with the impossibility result (Section 4) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible }k\\text{ are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\nBoth existence and maximality have been proved rigorously. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
