ogether with \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) give a family of exactly \\(n\\) lines covering all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma 4.1* gives the maximal number of lattice points of \\(S_n\\) that a single line can contain:\n   * a non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points,\n   * a sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points.\n\n   If a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\;+\\;k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Requiring this to be at least \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) yields the inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\). Hence no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 3.1 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realized; Lemma 4.1 shows that larger \\(k\\) are impossible. Therefore the admissible values are exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set that must be covered  \n\nFix an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A family of non‑sunny lines that always covers \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_t:=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le t-2\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{x=1,\\;x=2\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) consists of \\(t-2\\) horizontal lines and two vertical lines, altogether \\(t\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny.\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \n\\(\\mathcal N_t\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because