\((n+1-a)-(t-1)+1=n+2-t-a\\).  Summing over all admissible \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|U|\n&=\\sum_{a=3}^{\\,n+2-t}\\bigl(n+2-t-a\\bigr)\\\\\n&=\\sum_{r=1}^{\\,n-t-1} r\n   \\qquad\\bigl(r=n+2-t-a\\bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{(n-t-1)(n-t)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t=n-k\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|U|=\\frac{(k-1)k}{2}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nNow we treat the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\ge3\\)*, then \\(\\frac{(k-1)k}{2}\\ge k\\); consequently \\(|U|\\ge k\\) and we can select \\(k\\) distinct points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) from \\(U\\).  By construction each \\(P_i\\) lies outside every line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).\n\n*If \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=2\\)*, the inequality \\(|U|\\ge k\\) fails, but we can exhibit the required points directly:\n\n- For \\(k=1\\) (so \\(t=n-1\\)) the point \\((3,n-1)\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\) (since \\(3+(n-1)=n+2\\le n+1\\) is false; we must adjust).  Take \\((3, n-2)\\): we have \\(3+(n-2)=n+1\\), so \\((3,n-2)\\in S_n\\).  Its coordinates satisfy \\(a\\ge3\\) and \\(b=n-2\\ge t-1=n-2\\); hence it is not on any line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).\n\n- For \\(k=2\\) (so \\(t=n-2\\)) the two points \\((3,n-3)\\) and \\((4,n-3)\\) both belong to \\(S_n\\) and satisfy \\(a\\ge3,\\;b=n-3\\ge t-1=n-3\\); they are distinct and avoid all lines of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).\n\nThus **Lemma 3.1** holds:\n\n> **Lemma 3.1**  \n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist at least \\(k\\) points of \\(S_n\\) that are not incident with any line of \\(\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\nHaving obtained such points, label them \\(P_i=(a_i,b_i)\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,k)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction of the sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\) choose a slope \\(\\lambda_i\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\lambda_i\\neq\\lambda_j\\;(j<i).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nOnly finitely many slopes are forbidden, so this is always possible.  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-b_i=\\lambda_i(x-a_i).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is sunny (its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), passes through the prescribed point \\(P_i\\), and is distinct from all previously defined lines because its slope is new.  Moreover, a sunny line cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n:=\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n* \\(|\\mathcal H_n|=(n-k)+k=n\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are sunny;\n* by Lemma 2.1 every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\), and the additionally chosen points \\(P_i\\) lie on the sunny lines \\(L_i\\).  Hence **all** points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\n#### Lemma 5.1 (Maximum points on a line)\n\n* A non‑sunny line (slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)) meets the triangle \\(\\{a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in a segment whose projection on the \\(x\\)-axis (or on the \\(y\\)-axis) has length at most \\(n\\); therefore it contains at most \\(n\\) lattice points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* Let a sunny line have slope \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  Write \\(\\lambda=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  Two distinct lattice points on the line differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\); each step changes the sum \\(a+b\\) by \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\).  Since the sum can increase from the smallest to the largest point by at most \\(n\\), the line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### Upper‑bound inequality\n\nAssume a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  By Lemma 5.1 the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, we require  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward manipulation (consider separately the cases \\(n\\) even and odd) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThus no arrangement can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nLemma 3.1 together with the construction of Section 4 shows that every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\ncan be realized.  Lemma 5.1 and inequality (18) show that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible.  \n\nHence the complete answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible }k\\text{ are exactly }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\] ∎"
