            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set that must be covered  \n\nFix an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A family of non‑sunny lines that always covers \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_t:=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le t-2\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{x=1,\\;x=2\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) consists of \\(t-2\\) horizontal lines and two vertical lines, altogether \\(t\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny.\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \n\\(\\mathcal N_t\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(b\\le t-2\\).  \nThen the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal N_t\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b=t-1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+2-t\\). Since \\(t\\le n\\), we have \\(n+2-t\\ge2\\); consequently \\(a\\) can be either \\(1\\) or \\(2\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) or \\(a=2\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal N_t\\).  \nNo other value of \\(a\\) is possible, because \\(a\\ge3\\) would give \\(a+b\\ge3+(t-1)=t+2>n+1\\), contradicting \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\nThus every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to some line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\). ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently, for every integer  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad (2\\le t\\le n)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nthe family \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) provides a covering by exactly \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Adding sunny lines – existence of uncovered points\n\nLet \\(k\\) be a non‑negative integer with \\(k\\le n-2\\) and put \\(t=n-k\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).  \nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done: \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) already consists of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nAssume \\(k\\ge1\\).  We must exhibit at least \\(k\\) points of \\(S_n\\) that are **not** on any line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).\n\nRecall that \\(\\mathcal N_t\\) consists of the two vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) and the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,t-2\\).  \nHence a point \\((a,b)\\) is *outside* \\(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal N_t}L\\) precisely when  \n\n\\[\na\\ge 3\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\ge t-1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge3,\\;b\\ge t-1\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nWe now count \\(|U|\\).\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge t-1\\), we have \\(a\\le n+2-t\\).  \nThus \\(a\\) ranges from \\(3\\) to \\(n+2-t\\) (inclusive).  For a fixed \\(a\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are  \n\n\\[\nb=t-1,\\;t,\\;\\dots ,\\;n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nHence the number of choices for \\(b\\) equals \\((n+1-a)-(t-1)+1=n+2-t-a\\).  Summing over all admissible \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|U|\n&=\\sum_{a=3}^{\\,n+2-t}\\bigl(n+2-t-a\\bigr)\\\\\n&=\\sum_{r=1}^{\\,n-t-1} r\n   \\qquad\\bigl(r=n+2-t-a\\bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{(n-t-1)(n-t)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t=n-k\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|U|=\\frac{(k-1)k}{2}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nNow we treat the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\ge3\\)*, then \\(\\frac{(k-1)k}{2}\\ge k\\); consequently \\(|U|\\ge k\\) and we can select \\(k\\) distinct points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) from \\(U\\).  By construction each \\(P_i\\) lies outside every line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).\n\n*If \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=2\\)*, the inequality \\(|U|\\ge k\\) fails, but we can exhibit the required points directly:\n\n- For \\(k=1\\) (so \\(t=n-1\\)) the point \\((3,n-1)\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\) (since \\(3+(n-1)=n+2\\le n+1\\) is false; we must adjust).  Take \\((3, n-2)\\): we have \\(3+(n-2)=n+1\\), so \\((3,n-2)\\in S_n\\).  Its coordinates satisfy \\(a\\ge3\\) and \\(b=n-2\\ge t-1=n-2\\); hence it is not on any line of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).\n\n- For \\(k=2\\) (so \\(t=n-2\\)) the two points \\((3,n-3)\\) and \\((4,n-3)\\) both belong to \\(S_n\\) and satisfy \\(a\\ge3,\\;b=n-3\\ge t-1=n-3\\); they are distinct and avoid all lines of \\(\\mathcal N_t\\).\n\nThus **Lemma 3.1** holds:\n\n> **Lemma 3.1**  \n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist at least \\(k\\) points of \\(S_n\\) that are not incident with any line of \\(\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\nHaving obtained such points, label them \\(P_i=(a_i,b_i)\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,k)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction of the sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\) choose a slope \\(\\lambda_i\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\lambda_i\\neq\\lambda_j\\;(j<i).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nOnly finitely many slopes are forbidden, so this is always possible.  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-b_i=\\lambda_i(x-a_i).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is sunny (its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), passes through the prescribed point \\(P_i\\), and is distinct from all previously defined lines because its slope is new.  Moreover, a sunny line cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n:=\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n