et  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n:=\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n* \\(|\\mathcal H_n|=(n-k)+k=n\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are sunny;\n* by Lemma 2.1 every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N_{\\,n-k}\\), and the additionally chosen points \\(P_i\\) lie on the sunny lines \\(L_i\\).  \n  Hence **all** points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\n#### Lemma 5.1 (Maximum number of lattice points of \\(S_n\\) on a line)\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA line of slope \\(0\\) is of the form \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge1\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the possible abscissae satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); therefore at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) integer points lie on the line.  \nA line of slope \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) is \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); the ordinate satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\), again at most \\(n\\) points.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\ge2\\).  \nInside the triangle we need \\(d\\le n+1\\); the integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots,(d-1,1)\\), i.e. exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\) points.  \nHence any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet a sunny line have slope \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  Write \\(\\lambda=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  Two distinct lattice points on the line differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\).  Consequently the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\) when moving from one lattice point to the next along the line.  \nInside the region \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the smallest possible value of \\(a+b\\) for a point on the line is at least \\(2\\); the largest is at most \\(n+1\\).  Hence the number of lattice points on the line is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1)-2}{2}+1=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\nAssume a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 5.1 the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-k)\\,n}_{\\text{non‑sunny}} \\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{k\\Bigl(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)}_{\\text{sunny}} .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we require  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nWe treat the parity of \\(n\\) separately.\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) is even*, then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and (13) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2m(2m+1)}2\\le (2m-k)2m+k(m+1)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n2m^2+m\\le 4m^2-2mk+km+ k .\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\n0\\le 2m^2-(2m-1)k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2m-1>0\\), this inequality is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\le \\frac{2m^2}{2m-1}=m+\\frac{m}{2m-1}<m+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, \\(k\\le m=n/2\\).  Since \\(n/2\\le n-2\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\), we obtain the stronger bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd*, then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and (13) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2\\le (2m+1-k)(2m+1)+k(m+1)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n(2m+1)(m+1)\\le (2m+1)(2m+1)-k(2m+1)+k(m+1).\n\\]\n\nAfter cancelling \\((2m+1)(m+1)\\) from both sides we obtain  \n\n\\[\n0\\le (2m+1)^2-(2m+1)k+k(m+1)\n= (2m+1)^2-(2m)k .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nk\\le \\frac{(2m+1)^2}{2m}=2m+1+\\frac{1}{2m}\\;<\\;2m+2=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integer, \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nBoth parity cases give the uniform bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nLemma 3.1 shows that for every integer \\(