 \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=3,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the\n\\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2, x+y=4,\\dots ,x+y=n\\).  Again we obtain \\(n\\) lines.\n\nAll selected lines belong to \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\); consequently each of them is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, i.e. none is sunny.  Because every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of each family, it certainly lies on at least one of the selected lines.  Hence we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and let\n\\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) be a non‑sunny line.  \nAssume there exists a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that is covered **only** by \\(\\ell\\).\nThen there exists a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose any point \\(Q\\in T_n\\) with \\(Q\\neq P\\) that is covered by at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\); such a point exists because \\(|T_n|>1\\).\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{\\text{slope of }L\\mid L\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) is a finite set; it does not contain the three forbidden slopes\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (otherwise the corresponding line would be sunny).  \nPick a real number \\(m\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nm\\notin S\\cup\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(\\ell'\\) to be the unique line of slope \\(m\\) passing through \\(P\\).\nBecause of (2) the line \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and is not parallel to any line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\).\n\nThe point \\(P\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell'\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) different from \\(P\\) were already covered by lines other than \\(\\ell\\); they remain covered because we have not removed any of those lines.  In particular \\(Q\\) stays covered.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) satisfies the covering condition. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) from Lemma 1 (no sunny lines).\n\nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done.  \nAssume \\(k\\ge 1\\).  While the current configuration contains fewer than \\(k\\) sunny\nlines we perform the following step.\n\nBecause \\(|T_n|>n\\) (see (1)), at any moment there is a point of \\(T_n\\) that is\ncovered by exactly one line of the configuration (otherwise each of the \\(n\\) lines\nwould have to cover at least two distinct points, giving at most \\(2n\\) incidences,\ncontradicting the fact that the total number of incidences is at least \\(|T_n|>2n\\)\nfor \\(n\\ge 4\\); the case \\(n=3\\) can be checked directly).  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the unique line covering such a point \\(P\\).  If \\(\\ell\\) is already sunny we skip the step; otherwise we apply Lemma 2 to replace \\(\\ell\\) by a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\).  The number of sunny lines increases by one and the covering property is preserved.\n\nRepeating this operation we can increase the number of sunny lines one by one.\nSince the total number of lines is fixed at \\(n\\), we cannot perform more than\n\\(n-2\\) replacements: after \\(n-2\\) sunny lines have been created, at least two\nlines of the original (non‑sunny) family must remain, and those two are needed to\ncover the two points \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\) which lie on the extreme\nvertical and horizontal borders of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently the process can be\ncarried out for any prescribed \\(k\\le n-2\\), yielding a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\))\n\nNo configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) can contain \\(n-1\\) or\n\\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) with \\(n\\) lines\ncovers \\(T_n\\) and has at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Then at most one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny; denote it by \\(\\ell_0\\) (if all lines are sunny, set\n\\(\\ell_0\\) to be any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\)).\n\nConsider the two extreme points of \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n-1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)\nrespectively, which are forbidden directions.  Consequently any line that passes\nthrough \\(P_1\\) and is not vertical must have slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\nthe same holds for any line through \\(P_2\\) that is not horizontal.\n\nSince there is at most one non‑sunny line, at least one of the points \\(P_1,P_2\\)\nis covered solely by a sunny line.  Suppose \\(P_1\\) is covered only by a sunny\nline \\(\\ell_1\\).  Because \\(\\ell_1\\) is not vertical, it meets the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) only at \\(P_1\\).  Hence no other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\) with\nthe same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  The same reasoning applied to \\(P_2\\) shows that if\n\\(P_2\\) is covered only by a sunny line \\(\\ell_2\\), then \\(\\ell_2\\) is not\nhorizontal and meets the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) only at \\(P_2\\).\n\nIf both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) are covered by sunny lines, then those two sunny lines\nmust be distinct (they have different slopes, otherwise they would be parallel\nand could not pass through the two distinct points).  Consequently we would have\nat least two sunny lines that are **not** parallel to any forbidden direction and\nthat each contain exactly one point of \\(T_n\\).  All remaining \\(n-2\\) lines must\ncover the remaining \\(|T_n|-2\\) points.  However, each sunny line can intersect\nany other line in at most one point, so the total number of incidences between\nthe \\(n\\) lines and the points of \\(T_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{points covered uniquely})\\;+\\;(n-2)\\cdot 2\\;=\\;2n-2,\n\\]\n\nwhere the factor \\(2\\) for the non‑sunny lines comes from the fact that a line\nparallel to a forbidden direction can contain at most two points of the\ntriangular set \\(T_n\\) (one on each of the two bordering sides).  For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 < \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}=|T_n|,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the requirement that every point of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  Hence a\nconfiguration with \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemmas 1–3 show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.  \nLemma 4 proves that no configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\] ∎"
