\dots ,x=n-2\\),\nthe \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) horizontal lines \\(y=2, y=4,\\dots ,y=n-1\\), and the single diagonal\nline \\(x+y=n\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*: take the \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=3,\\dots ,x=n-1\\)\nand the \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2, x+y=4,\\dots ,x+y=n\\).\n\nAll chosen lines belong to \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\); consequently none\nis sunny.  Because each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of each family,\nit certainly lies on at least one of the selected lines.  Hence we have a\nconfiguration \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacing a uniquely covering line by a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).\nAssume that a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) contains a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that is not\ncontained in any other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\nThen there exists a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{\\text{slope of }L\\mid L\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) is a finite set and does not contain the three forbidden slopes\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (otherwise the corresponding line would be sunny).  \nChoose a real number \\(m\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm\\notin S\\cup\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(\\ell'\\) to be the unique line of slope \\(m\\) passing through \\(P\\).\nBecause of (2) the line \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and is not parallel to any line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\).\n\nThe point \\(P\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell'\\).  Every other point of \\(T_n\\) was\ncovered by a line different from \\(\\ell\\) and therefore remains covered after the\nreplacement.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) satisfies the required covering property. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) from Lemma 1 (no sunny lines).\nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done.\n\nAssume we have already constructed a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) with\n\\(s<k\\) sunny lines.  Because of (1) we have \\(|T_n|>n\\); therefore among the\n\\(n\\) lines at least one of them covers a point of \\(T_n\\) that no other line\ncovers.  (Indeed, if every point were covered by at least two lines, the total\nnumber of incidences would be at least \\(2|T_n|>2n\\), contradicting the fact that\neach line contributes at most \\(|T_n|\\) incidences and there are only \\(n\\) lines.)\n\nSelect such a uniquely covered point \\(P\\) and let \\(\\ell\\) be the line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) that contains it.  If \\(\\ell\\) is already sunny we leave the\nconfiguration unchanged; otherwise we apply Lemma 2 to replace \\(\\ell\\) by a sunny\nline \\(\\ell'\\).  The new configuration still has \\(n\\) distinct lines, still\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\), and its number of sunny lines has increased by one.\n\nRepeating this operation we can raise the number of sunny lines from \\(0\\) up to\n\\(k\\).  The process stops after at most \\(k\\) replacements, and because we never\nneed more than \\(n-2\\) replacements (the bound proved in Lemma 4), it is always\npossible for any \\(k\\le n-2\\).  The resulting configuration is the desired\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\nIn any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) the number of\nsunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the leftmost column of points  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery line that is not vertical meets this column in **at most one** point,\nbecause two distinct points of the column have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate and a\nnon‑vertical line can intersect a given vertical line only once.  Hence to cover\nall \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\) we need at least \\(n-1\\) lines that are either\nvertical (non‑sunny) or sunny lines each covering a distinct point of the column.\n\nThe same reasoning applies to the rightmost column  \n\n\\[\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, it is impossible that both columns are\ncovered exclusively by sunny lines; at least one vertical line is required for\none of the columns.  Consequently **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be\nnon‑sunny (one vertical line for each of the two extreme columns, or a single\nvertical line that serves both columns together, which still leaves at least one\nadditional non‑sunny line needed to cover the opposite extreme column).  \n\nTherefore the number of sunny lines can be at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 provides a configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \nLemma 4 proves that no configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\] ∎"
