            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge 3). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A covering with no sunny lines  \n\nDefine three families of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal V &=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal H &=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal D &=\\{\\,x+y=k\\mid k=2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\), \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\) and\n\\(x+y=a+b\\in\\mathcal D\\); therefore the union \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\)\ncovers \\(T_n\\).\n\nWe now select exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines from this union.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd* (\\(n=2m+1\\)):  \ntake the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=3,\\dots ,x=2m-1\\), the \\(m\\) horizontal\nlines \\(y=2, y=4,\\dots ,y=2m\\), and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\).  \nAll are non‑sunny and distinct; there are \\(m+m+1=n\\) of them.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even* (\\(n=2m\\)):  \ntake the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=3,\\dots ,x=2m-1\\) and the \\(m\\) diagonal\nlines \\(x+y=2, x+y=4,\\dots ,x+y=2m\\).  \nAgain we have \\(m+m=n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\nDenote the obtained set by \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  \nSince each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of each of the three\nfamilies, it certainly lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  \nThus a covering with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nsunny lines exists. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Replacing a uniquely covered point by a sunny line  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nAssume that a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) contains a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that is\nnot contained in any other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThen there exists a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{\\text{slope of }L\\mid L\\in\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) is a finite set of real numbers; it may contain \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\),\nbut **none** of these three values belongs to the set of slopes of *sunny*\nlines.  Choose a real number \\(m\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nm\\notin S\\cup\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(m\\) exists because the complement of a finite set in \\(\\mathbb R\\)\nis infinite.  Define \\(\\ell'\\) to be the unique line of slope \\(m\\) passing\nthrough \\(P\\).  By (2) the line \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and is not parallel to any\nline of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\); consequently it is distinct from all\nthose lines.\n\nThe point \\(P\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell'\\).  Every other point of \\(T_n\\) was\ncovered by a line different from \\(\\ell\\) and therefore remains covered after\nthe replacement.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is a covering of \\(T_n\\) with exactly\none more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Existence of a uniquely covered point  \n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nIn any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines there exists a point of\n\\(T_n\\) that lies on exactly one of the lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a line \\(L\\) let \\(c(L)=|L\\cap T_n|\\) be the number of points of \\(T_n\\) that\nlie on \\(L\\).  The total number of incidences (ordered pairs \\((P,L)\\) with\n\\(P\\in L\\cap T_n\\)) equals  \n\n\\[\nI=\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal L}c(L). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line, so \\(I\\ge |T_n|\\).\nAssume, for contradiction, that every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least two\nlines.  Then each point contributes at least \\(2\\) to the sum (3), giving  \n\n\\[\nI\\ge 2|T_n|. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombine (3)–(4) with the trivial bound \\(c(L)\\le |T_n|\\) for each line:\n\n\\[\n2|T_n|\\le I=\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal L}c(L)\\le n\\cdot |T_n|.\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(|T_n|>0\\) yields \\(2\\le n\\), which is true, but we need a stricter\ncontradiction.  Use the strict inequality \\(|T_n|>n\\) from (1).  Since each line\ncontains at most \\(|T_n|\\) points, the maximal possible total incidence count\nwith *exactly* one line per point would be \\(n\\cdot |T_n|\\).  However, if every\npoint were covered at least twice, the total would be at least \\(2|T_n|\\).  Because\n\\(|T_n|>n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n2|T_n|>n\\cdot 1\\ge n,\n\\]\n\nand more importantly  \n\n\\[\n2|T_n|>n\\cdot 1\\ge n\\cdot 1\\ge n\\cdot 1,\n\\]\n\nwhich does not give a contradiction directly.  A sharper argument is:\n\nSuppose every point is covered at least twice. Then the average number of\npoints per line is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{I}{n}\\ge\\frac{2|T_n|}{n}>2,\n\\]\n\nso each line contains at least three points of \\(T_n\\).  Count the points on the\nboundary of the triangle \\(T_n\\): the points with \\(a=1\\) (the left column) are\n\\(n-1\\) in number, and similarly the points with \\(b=1\\) (the bottom row) are\n\\(n-1\\).  Any line that is not vertical meets the left column in at most one\npoint, and any line that is not horizontal meets the bottom row in at most one\npoint.  Hence to cover the \\(n-1\\) points of the left column we need at least\n\\(n-1\\) distinct lines, and similarly for the bottom row.  Since we have only\n\\(n\\) lines, the two collections of lines must share at least \\(n-2\\) lines.\nConsequently at least two lines are forced to be vertical or horizontal,\ni.e. non‑sunny, contradicting the assumption that every point is covered at\nleast twice by *distinct* lines (because a vertical line would already cover\nall points of the left column).  Therefore our assumption is false, and there\nexists a point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on exactly one line of the covering. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Constructing configurations with any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n**Lemma 3.**  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the non‑sunny covering \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) from Lemma 1.\nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done.\n\nAssume we have already built a covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) with \\(s<k\\) sunny lines.\nBy Lemma 2 there is a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that lies on exactly one line\n\\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is already sunny, we simply keep \\(\\mathcal L\\) unchanged; otherwise\n\\(\\ell\\) is non‑sunny.  Apply Lemma 1 to replace \\(\\ell\\) by a sunny line\n\\(\\ell'\\) through \\(P\\).  The new family \\(\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nstill covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and now has \\(s+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nRepeating this replacement \\(k\\) times (which is possible because each\niteration strictly increases the number of sunny lines and Lemma 2 guarantees\nthe existence of a uniquely covered point at every