cover any point\n  outside the two columns, contradicting the requirement that every point of\n  \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\n- The symmetric situation with the vertical line at \\(x=n-1\\) leads to the same\n  contradiction.\n\nHence at least two lines must be non‑sunny (they can be vertical, horizontal\nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number of\nsunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 provides a covering with \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a covering\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \nLemma 4 proves that no covering can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
