ottom row.  Since we have only\n\\(n\\) lines, the two collections of lines must share at least \\(n-2\\) lines.\nConsequently at least two lines are forced to be vertical or horizontal,\ni.e. non‑sunny, contradicting the assumption that every point is covered at\nleast twice by *distinct* lines (because a vertical line would already cover\nall points of the left column).  Therefore our assumption is false, and there\nexists a point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on exactly one line of the covering. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Constructing configurations with any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n**Lemma 3.**  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the non‑sunny covering \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) from Lemma 1.\nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done.\n\nAssume we have already built a covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) with \\(s<k\\) sunny lines.\nBy Lemma 2 there is a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that lies on exactly one line\n\\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is already sunny, we simply keep \\(\\mathcal L\\) unchanged; otherwise\n\\(\\ell\\) is non‑sunny.  Apply Lemma 1 to replace \\(\\ell\\) by a sunny line\n\\(\\ell'\\) through \\(P\\).  The new family \\(\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nstill covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and now has \\(s+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nRepeating this replacement \\(k\\) times (which is possible because each\niteration strictly increases the number of sunny lines and Lemma 2 guarantees\nthe existence of a uniquely covered point at every step) yields a covering\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n**Lemma 4.**  \nIn any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines, at least two of the lines\nmust be non‑sunny; consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the two extreme vertical columns of points:\n\n\\[\nC_{1}=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\},\\qquad\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth columns contain exactly \\(n-1\\) points.\n\n*Observation.* A sunny line is neither vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\); in\nparticular it is not vertical.  Hence a sunny line meets a given vertical\ncolumn in **at most one** point (otherwise two points of the same column would\nhave the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical).\n\nTherefore, to cover all points of \\(C_{1}\\) we need at least \\(n-1\\) lines,\neach of which either is vertical (non‑sunny) or is a sunny line covering a\ndifferent point of the column.  The same statement holds for \\(C_{n-1}\\).\n\nSuppose, aiming for a contradiction, that at most one line among the whole\nfamily is non‑sunny.  Then at most one vertical line exists.  That single\nvertical line can cover at most one of the two columns completely; the other\ncolumn would then have to be covered entirely by sunny lines, requiring\n\\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines.  Adding the possible vertical line gives at most\n\\(n\\) lines, but we would need \\(n-1\\) sunny lines **plus** the vertical line,\ni.e. \\(n\\) lines, leaving no line to cover the remaining points of the first\ncolumn.  A direct counting shows impossibility:\n\n- If the vertical line is placed at \\(x=1\\), it covers all points of \\(C_{1}\\).\n  Then \\(C_{n-1}\\) must be covered by sunny lines, requiring \\(n-1\\) distinct\n  lines of slope different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\).  Together with the vertical\n  line we already have \\(n\\) lines, leaving **no** line to cover any point\n  outside the two columns, contradicting the requirement that every point of\n  \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\n- The symmetric situation with the vertical line at \\(x=n-1\\) leads to the same\n  contradiction.\n\nHence at least two lines must be non‑sunny (they can be vertical, horizontal\nor of s