\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge 3). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A covering with no sunny lines  \n\nDefine the following families of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal V &=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal D_1&=\\{\\,x+y=n\\,\\},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal D_2&=\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D_1\\cup\\mathcal D_2\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\((n-2)+2=n\\) distinct lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny\n(vertical or diagonal).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n  \u2013 If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(b=2\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nsunny lines exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nConsider the two extreme vertical columns of \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\nC_{1}=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\},\\qquad\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach column contains \\(n-1\\) points.\n\n* A **vertical** line contains all points of a column.\n* Any **non\u2011vertical** line meets a given vertical column in at most one point\n  (two points of the same column have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the line\n  to be vertical).\n\nHence, to cover a column we need either one vertical line or at least \\(n-