{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which\nexactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 4.3  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) still covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If the vertical line \\(x=a\\) has **not** been removed*, then \\((a,b)\\) is still on that vertical line, so it is covered.\n\n*If the vertical line \\(x=a\\) **has** been removed* (i.e. \\(a=j_{t}\\) for some\n\\(t\\)), then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the column \\(x=j_{t}\\).  \nTwo possibilities occur:\n\n1. **\\(b=b_{t}\\).**  The point \\((j_{t},b_{t})\\) lies on the newly added sunny line\n   \\(\\ell_{t}\\), so it is covered.\n\n2. **\\(b\\neq b_{t}\\).**  The sum \\(a+b=j_{t}+b\\) equals either \\(n\\) or \\(n+1\\)\n   because every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and the two diagonal\n   lines \\(x+y=n\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) are still present in \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of those two diagonal lines, which are unchanged,\n   and is therefore covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\nLemma\u202f4 provides, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit\nconfiguration attaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Therefore the set of all\nadmissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
