ve exhibited a configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No configuration can contain a sunny line\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least one sunny line.  Let \\(L_s\\in\\mathcal F\\) be a sunny line.\n\n#### 2.1  Points that a sunny line can cover\n\nTwo distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the same diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) have slope \\(-1\\) between them.  Since a sunny line is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it cannot contain two points with the same sum of coordinates.  Hence \\(L_s\\) contains at most one point from each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(t=2,\\dots ,n\\)).  There are \\(n-1\\) such diagonals, so\n\n\\[\n|L_s\\cap T_n|\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2  Points that the remaining lines can cover\n\nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are not sunny; each is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n*If a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis* (i.e. horizontal), it has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  Inside \\(T_n\\) the possible values of \\(b\\) are \\(1,\\dots ,n\\); therefore such a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If a line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis* (vertical), an analogous bound of \\(n\\) points holds.\n\n*If a line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)*, it is precisely a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) for some integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).  The diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) contains exactly \\(t-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the pairs \\((1,t-1),\\dots ,(t-1,1)\\)).\n\nConsequently, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines is at most\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=2}^{n}(t-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(The sum (3) is attained when each of the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines is a distinct diagonal; any horizontal or vertical line would only reduce the total.)\n\n#### 2.3  Overall bound\n\nAdding the contributions of the sunny line (2) and of the other lines (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\n|\\,\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F} (L\\cap T_n)\\,|\n\\le (n-1)+\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\n= \\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut by (1) the set \\(T_n\\) itself has cardinality \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} \\;-\\; \\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}=1>0,\n\\]\n\nso (4) is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|\\).  Hence the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nTherefore **no** configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement can contain a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Section 1 we have exhibited a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.  \nFrom Section 2 we have proved that any configuration containing at least one sunny line is impossible.  \n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
