ctly \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Points on the remaining lines\n\nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are not sunny; each is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n*Horizontal lines.* A line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge1\\)) meets \\(T_n\\) only at points with second coordinate \\(c\\); at most \\(n\\) such points exist.\n\n*Vertical lines.* Similarly, a line \\(x=c\\) meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).* Such a line is precisely a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).  The diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) contains exactly \\(t-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the pairs \\((1,t-1),\\dots ,(t-1,1)\\)).\n\nConsequently, regardless of the mixture of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines, the total number of **distinct** points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines is bounded above by the sum of the contributions of the diagonals, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{\\substack{L\\in\\mathcal F\\\\ L\\neq L_s}}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le\\sum_{t=2}^{n}(t-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(The bound is maximal when each of those lines is a distinct diagonal; any horizontal or vertical line would replace a diagonal and could only reduce the total, because a diagonal already accounts for all points on its own diagonal.)\n\n#### 2.3  Combining the contributions\n\nEvery point of \\(L_s\\cap T_n\\) lies on a unique diagonal \\(x+y=t\\); therefore those points are already counted in the sum (3).  Hence the total number of distinct points of \\(T_n\\) covered by the whole family satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.4  Contradiction\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}= \\frac{n}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\nso the bound (4) is **strictly smaller** than the required total \\(|T_n|\\).  Thus the assumed family