oints of \\(T_n\\) (if such a diagonal exists) or by a diagonal that contains at least as many of those points.  \n   - A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (with \\(t\\ge c+1\\)) in exactly one point, so it contributes at most one point from each of the \\(n-c\\) diagonals it meets, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points in total.  \n   - The diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with the same \\(t\\) contains **all** points of \\(T_n\\) lying on that diagonal, namely \\(t-1\\) points, which is never smaller than the contribution of the horizontal line to that diagonal.  \n   An analogous argument holds for vertical lines. Therefore replacing a horizontal or vertical line by a suitable diagonal never decreases the total number of covered points. Repeating this replacement yields a configuration consisting solely of diagonals that attains the maximal possible total, which is exactly the sum in (2). \u220e\n\n   Applying Lemma\u202f3 with \\(m=n-1\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L_s\\}}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n   \\le\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the points contributed by the sunny line and using (1) we get an overall bound for the whole family:\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n   \\le\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}+(n-1)=\\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   From (1) we have \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  For every \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n   because the difference equals \\(\\dfrac{2}{2}=1\\).  Hence the bound (4) is strictly smaller than the required total number of points, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Therefore **no** covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain a sunny line.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction in step\u202f2 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable, while step\u202f4 proves that any \\(k\\ge 1\\) is impossible.  Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is obtained by summing, for each admissible \\(a\\),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines have different constants \\(t\\); the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is not a diagonal, so all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny.*  \nEach diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); the line \\(y=1\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis.  Hence none of the lines is sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of any sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least one sunny line.  Let \\(L_s\\in\\mathcal F\\) be a sunny line.\n\n#### Lemma\u202f1  \nTwo distinct points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the same diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) have slope \\(-1\\).  Since a sunny line is **not