nfiguration cannot cover more points than this diagonal configuration. \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f3 with \\(m=n-1\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L_s\\}}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAdding the points contributed by the sunny line and using (6),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}+(n-1)=\\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n#### Contradiction\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause the difference equals \\(\\dfrac{2}{2}=1>0\\).  Hence the bound (9) is strictly smaller than the required total number of points, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTherefore a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain any sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of admissible \\(k\\)\n\n- Existence for \\(k=0\\) is given by the construction in Section\u202f1.  \n- Non\u2011existence for any \\(k\\ge1\\) follows from the contradiction proved in Section\u202f2.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3.}\n\\]"
