            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct and non‑sunny (the diagonals are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); \\(y=1\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\):\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(y=1\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of any sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least one sunny line.  Choose a sunny line \\(L_s\\in\\mathcal F\\).\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal \\(x+y=t\\); therefore it meets each such diagonal in at most one point.  Since there are \\(n-1\\) diagonals with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\n|L_s\\cap T_n|\\le n-1. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### Lemma 2 (size of a non‑sunny line)\n\nFor a non‑sunny line \\(L\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{if }L:y=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n), &&|L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c;\\\\\n&\\text{if }L:x=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n), &&|L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c;\\\\\n&\\text{if }L:x+y=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n), &&|L\\cap T_n|=t-1.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese counts follow directly from the definition of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### Lemma 3 (maximal coverage by non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(m\\le n-1\\) and let \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) be distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nDenote \\(S=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}(L_i\\cap T_n)\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (8) the possible cardinalities of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) are the integers  \n\n\\[\nn,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\dots ,2 .\n\\]\n\nTo maximise \\(|S|\\) we should select the \\(m\\) lines with the largest individual sizes, because replacing any chosen line by another line whose intersection size is not larger cannot increase \\(|S|\\).  \nThe \\(m\\) largest sizes are obtained by the following collection:\n\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (size \\(n\\)) – we may take it if \\(m\\ge1\\);\n* the diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=m\\) (sizes \\(1,2,\\dots ,m-1\\)).\n\nAll other non‑sunny lines have intersection sizes \\(\\le m-1\\) and therefore cannot improve the total.\n\nThe chosen lines intersect only at the points where a diagonal meets the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); namely the point \\((t-1,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=t\\).  Consequently the number of *distinct* points covered is\n\n\\[\nn\\;+\\;\\sum_{t=2}^{m}\\bigl[(t-1)-1\\bigr]\n= n+\\sum_{t=2}^{m}(t-2)\n= n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich proves (9). ∎\n\nApply Lemma 3 with \\(m=n-1\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines when a sunny line is present).  Using (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L_s\\}}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the sunny line and using (7),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le \\Bigl(n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\Bigr)+(n-1)\n= \\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Contradiction\n\nFrom (6) we have \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|.\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (11) is strictly smaller than the total number of points that must be covered, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain any sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Determination of admissible \\(k\\)\n\n* The construction in Section 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \n* The contradiction proved in Sections 2–3 shows that any \\(k\\ge1\\) is impossible.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
