tc.\n   - Proving that the greedy choice “\\(y=1\\) together with the diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=m\\)” indeed yields the maximal total, because any other non‑sunny line either has a smaller individual size or overlaps the already chosen lines in at least as many points.\n\n   The resulting bound (9) is then used together with Lemma 1 to obtain an overall bound (11) that is one point short of \\(|T_n|\\).\n\n4. **Contradiction** – the bound shows that a family containing a sunny line cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\). Hence any covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain **no** sunny line.\n\n5. **Conclusion** – \\(k=0\\) is possible (construction) and \\(k\\ge1\\) is impossible (contradiction). Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is obtained by summing, for each integer \\(a\\) from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\), the admissible values of \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct and non‑sunny (the diagonals are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); \\(y=1\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=1\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of any sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least one sunny line.  Choose a sunny line \\(L_s\\in\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).  Therefore it meets each such diagonal in at most one point, and consequently  \n\n\\[\n|L_s\\cap T_n|\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  There are exactly \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\); each contributes at most one point of \\(T_n\\) to the intersection with \\(L_s\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (size of a non‑sunny line)\n\nFor a non‑sunny line \\(L\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{if }L:y=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n), &&|L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c;\\\\\n&\\text{if }L:x=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n), &&|L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c;\\\\\n&\\text{if }L:x+y=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n), &&|L\\cap T_n|=t-1.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese formulas follow directly from the definition of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (maximal number of points covered by \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(m\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le m\\le n-1\\) and let \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) be distinct non‑sunny lines.  Set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\bigl(L_i\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (3) the possible cardinalities of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) for a non‑sunny line are the integers  \n\n\\[\nn,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\dots ,2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe first show that among all families of \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines, the **maximum** of \\(|S|\\) is attained when the lines are\n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=m .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Step 1 – ordering.*  \nArrange the chosen lines so that  \n\n\\[\n|L_1\\cap T_n|\\ge |L_2\\cap T_n|\\ge\\cdots\\ge |L_m\\cap T_n|.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|L_1\\cap T_n|\\) is the largest possible size among all non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(|L_1\\cap T_n|=n\\) (realised by \\(y=1\\) or \\(x=1\\)).  If \\(L_1\\) were not a line of size \\(n\\), we could replace it by such a line; the replacement would not decrease \\(|S|\\) because the new line contains at least the same points as the old one (it contains \\(n\\) points, the old line contains at most \\(n\\)).  Hence we may assume \\(L_1\\) is \\(y=1\\) (or \\(x=1\\); the two are symmetric).\n\n*Step 2 – the second line.*  \nConsider any line \\(L\\) distinct from \\(y=1\\).  Its intersection with \\(y=1\\) is at most one point of \\(T_n\\) (the point where the two lines meet).  Therefore the number of **new** points contributed by adding \\(L\\) to the family \\(\\{y=1\\}\\) is at most \\(|L\\cap T_n|-1\\).  To maximise the total number of distinct points after two lines, we must choose a line that maximises \\(|L\\cap T_n|-1\\).  From (3) the quantity \\(|L\\cap T_n|-1\\) attains its maximum value \\(1\\) when \\(L\\) is the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) (size \\(1\\)).  Any other non‑sunny line has \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\le n-1\\), so \\(|L\\cap T_n|-1\\le n-2\\), but note that such a line would already intersect \\(y=1\\) in at most one point, and its *new* points would be at most \\(|L\\cap T_n|-1\\le n-2\\).  However, the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) contributes exactly one new point, namely \\((1,1)\\), and this is **not** already on \\(y=1\\) (since \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) as well).  To see which choice yields a larger total, we compare the two possibilities:\n\n* Using a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(t\\ge2\\) adds \\(t-1\\) points, of which exactly one coincides with \\(y=1\\) (the point \\((t-1,1)\\)).  Hence the number of *new* points is \\((t-1)-1=t-2\\).\n\n* Using a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge2\\) adds \\(n+1-c\\) points, all of which are distinct from those on \\(y=1\\) except possibly the point \\((1,c)\\) when \\(c=1\\) (which does not occur).  Thus the number of new points is \\(n+1-c\\).\n\nBecause \\(t-2\\le n-2\\) for all admissible \\(t\\) and \\(n+1-c\\le n-1\\) for all admissible \\(c\\), the **largest** possible number of new points after the first line is achieved by taking the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) (which adds exactly one new point).  Consequently we may assume \\(L_2\\) is \\(x+y=2\\).\n\n*Step 3 – induction.*  \nAssume that for some \\(r\\) with \\(2\\le r<m\\) we have already selected the lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=r .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines cover  \n\n\\[\nn+\\sum_{t=2}^{r}\\bigl[(t-1)-1\\bigr]\n= n+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ndistinct points of \\(T_n\\) (the sum counts, for each diagonal, the points not already on \\(y=1\\)).  \n\nNow consider adding a \\((r+1)\\)-st line \\(L\\) distinct from the lines in (8).  \nLet \\(p\\) be the number of points of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) that already belong to the set covered by (8).  \nSince each of the already chosen lines is either \\(y=1\\) or a diagonal, any point of \\(T_n\\) lies on **at most one** of the diagonals and on at most one horizontal line.  Hence a new line can intersect the already covered set in at most \\(r\\) points (one with each of the previously chosen lines).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\np\\le r. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of distinct points after adding \\(L\\) is therefore at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(n+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}\\Bigr)+\\bigl(|L\\cap T_n|-p\\bigr)\n\\le\n\\Bigl(n+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}\\Bigr)+\\bigl(|L\\cap T_n|-r\\bigr). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nTo maximise the right‑hand side we must maximise \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\).  By (3) the largest possible size of a non‑sunny line not already in (8) is \\(n\\) (realised by the other axis line \\(x=1\\)), but such a line would intersect each of the \\(r\\) already chosen lines in a distinct point, giving \\(p=r\\); consequently \\(|L\\cap T_n|-p\\le n-r\\).  However, the diagonal \\(x+y=r+1\\) has size \\(|L\\cap T_n|=r\\) and meets each of the previous \\(r\\) lines in exactly one point, namely the points \\((t-1,1)\\) for \\(2\\le t\\le r\\) and \\((r,1)\\) for the new diagonal; thus \\(p=r-1\\) and \\(|L\\cap T_n|-p=1\\).  Adding this diagonal therefore increases the total by exactly one, yielding  \n\n\\[\nn+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}+1\n= n+\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nAny other admissible line gives an increase of at most the same amount, because its contribution \\(|L\\cap T_n|-p\\) is bounded by the right‑hand side of (11) with \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\le n\\) and \\(p\\ge r\\).  Hence the choice \\(L_{r+1}=x+y=r+1\\) is optimal.\n\nBy induction, the optimal family of \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines is precisely the set (6).  For this family the number of distinct covered points is computed in (9) with \\(r=m-1\\), giving exactly the bound (4). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the contradiction\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing at least one sunny line \\(L_s\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑sunny; applying Lemma 3 with \\(m=n-1\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L_s\\}}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the sunny line and using Lemma 1 (2),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le \\Bigl(n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\Bigr)+(n-1)\n= \\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1 = |T_n|-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) can cover at most \\(|T_n|-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the requirement that every point of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  Hence a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain any sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of admissible \\(k\\)\n\n* The construction of Section 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n* The contradiction proved above shows that any covering family must have \\(k=0\\); therefore \\(k\\ge1\\) is impossible.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3.}\n\\]"
