=1\\) (the point \\((t-1,1)\\)).  Hence the number of *new* points is \\((t-1)-1=t-2\\).\n\n* Using a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge2\\) adds \\(n+1-c\\) points, all of which are distinct from those on \\(y=1\\) except possibly the point \\((1,c)\\) when \\(c=1\\) (which does not occur).  Thus the number of new points is \\(n+1-c\\).\n\nBecause \\(t-2\\le n-2\\) for all admissible \\(t\\) and \\(n+1-c\\le n-1\\) for all admissible \\(c\\), the **largest** possible number of new points after the first line is achieved by taking the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) (which adds exactly one new point).  Consequently we may assume \\(L_2\\) is \\(x+y=2\\).\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 induction.*  \nAssume that for some \\(r\\) with \\(2\\le r<m\\) we have already selected the lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=r .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines cover  \n\n\\[\nn+\\sum_{t=2}^{r}\\bigl[(t-1)-1\\bigr]\n= n+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ndistinct points of \\(T_n\\) (the sum counts, for each diagonal, the points not already on \\(y=1\\)).  \n\nNow consider adding a \\((r+1)\\)-st line \\(L\\) distinct from the lines in (8).  \nLet \\(p\\) be the number of points of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) that already belong to the set covered by (8).  \nSince each of the already chosen lines is either \\(y=1\\) or a diagonal, any point of \\(T_n\\) lies on **at most one** of the diagonals and on at most one horizontal line.  Hence a new line can intersect the already covered set in at most \\(r\\) points (one with each of the previously chosen lines).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\np\\le r. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of distinct points after adding \\(L\\) is therefore at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(n+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}\\Bigr)+\\bigl(|L\\cap T_n|-p\\bigr)\n\\le\n\\Bigl(n+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}\\Bigr)+\\bigl(|L\\cap T_n|-r\\bigr). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nTo maximise the right\u2011hand side we must maximise \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\).  By (3) the largest possible size of a non\u2011sunny line not already in (8) is \\(n\\) (realised by the other axis line \\(x=1\\)), but such a line would intersect each of the \\(r\\) already chosen lines in a distinct point, giving \\(p=r\\); consequently \\(|L\\cap T_n|-p\\le n-r\\).  However, the diagonal \\(x+y=r+1\\) has size \\(|L\\cap T_n|=r\\) and meets each of the previous \\(r\\) lines in exactly one point, namely the points \\((t-1,1)\\) for \\(2\\le t\\le r\\) and \\((r,1)\\) for the new diagonal; thus \\(p=r-1\\) and \\(|L\\cap T_n|-p=1\\).  Adding this diagonal therefore increases the total by exactly one, yielding  \n\n\\[\nn+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}+1\n= n+\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nAny other admissible line gives an increase of at most the same amount, because its contribution \\(|L\\cap T_n|-p\\) is bounded by the right\u2011hand side of (11) with \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\le n\\) and \\(p\\ge r\\).  Hence the choice \\(L_{r+1}=x+y=r+1\\) is optimal.\n\nBy induction, the optimal family of \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines is precisely the set (6).  For this family the number of distinct covered points is computed in (9) with \\(r=m-1\\), giving exactly the bound (4). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the contradiction\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing at least one sunny line \\(L_s\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; applying Lemma\u202f3 with \\(m=n-1\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L_s\\}}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the sunny line and using Lemma\u202f1 (2),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le \\Bigl(n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\Bigr)+(n-1)\n= \\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1 = |T_n|-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) can cover at most \\(|T_n|-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the requirement that every point of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  Hence a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain any sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of admissible \\(k\\)\n\n* The construction of Section\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n* The contradiction proved above shows that any covering family must have \\(k=0\\); therefore \\(k\\ge1\\) is impossible.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3.}\n\\]"

