indeed attains the bound (4). Any other choice cannot give more new points because every non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or a diagonal, and each of these types adds at most the same number of new points as the corresponding line in the greedy family.\n\n   Using Lemma 1 for the sunny line and Lemma 3 with \\(m=n-1\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n   \\le\\Bigl(n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\Bigr)+(n-1)\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1=|T_n|-1,\n   \\]\n   contradicting the requirement that all points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\n4. **Conclusion** – a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a sunny line; therefore the only possible number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is obtained by summing, for each integer \\(a\\) from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\), the admissible values of \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\bigl\\{\\,y=1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct and non‑sunny (the diagonals are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); \\(y=1\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=1\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of any sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least one sunny line.  Choose a sunny line \\(L_s\\in\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).  Therefore it meets each such diagonal in at most one point, and consequently  \n\n\\[\n|L_s\\cap T_n|\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  There are exactly \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\); each contributes at most one point of \\(T_n\\) to the intersection with \\(L_s\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (size of the intersection of a non‑sunny line)\n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or a diagonal parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  From the definition of \\(T_n\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&L:y=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)      &&\\Longrightarrow &&|L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c,\\\\\n&L:x=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)      &&\\Longrightarrow &&|L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c,\\\\\n&L:x+y=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n)    &&\\Longrightarrow &&|L\\cap T_n|=t-1.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (maximal number of points covered by \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(m\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le m\\le n-1\\) and let \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) be distinct non‑sunny lines.  Set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\bigl(L_i\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe prove that the bound (4) is attained by the specific family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_m=\\bigl\\{\\,y=1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le m\\,\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nand that no other choice of \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines can cover more points.\n\n**Step 1 – a greedy extremal principle.**  \nFor any non‑sunny line \\(L\\) denote by \\(\\Delta(L)\\) the number of *new* points that \\(L\\) contributes when it is added to a family already chosen.  Clearly \\(\\Delta(L)=|L\\cap T_n|-p\\), where \\(p\\) is the number of points of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) already belonging to the previously selected lines.\n\nWe start with the empty family.  Among all non‑sunny lines the maximal possible size \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\) is \\(n\\) (realised by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) or the vertical line \\(x=1\\)).  Adding such a line therefore yields the largest possible \\(\\Delta\\) for the first step.  Hence we may assume that the first line is \\(y=1\\).\n\n**Step 2 – the second line.**  \nSuppose the line \\(y=1\\) has already been chosen.  Any other non‑sunny line meets \\(y=1\\) in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently, for a line \\(L\\) distinct from \\(y=1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\Delta(L)\\le |L\\cap T_n|-1.\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) the quantity \\(|L\\cap T_n|-1\\) equals\n\n* \\(n-2\\) for a horizontal or vertical line different from \\(y=1\\);\n* \\(t-2\\) for a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).\n\nThe largest value among these possibilities is \\(n-2\\), attained by a horizontal or vertical line.  However, such a line would already contain \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\), all of which (except possibly one) are *new* with respect to \\(y=1\\).  In contrast, the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) has \\(|L\\cap T_n|=1\\) and shares its unique point \\((1,1)\\) with \\(y=1\\); thus \\(\\Delta=0\\).  Since we are interested in **maximising the total number of distinct points after all \\(m\\) lines are chosen**, we must compare the *cumulative* effect, not the single‑step gain.  The crucial observation is that a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (with \\(t\\ge3\\)) adds exactly \\(t-2\\) new points, which is **strictly smaller** than the number of new points contributed by any horizontal/vertical line (\\(\\ge n-2\\)).  Nevertheless, a horizontal/vertical line would also intersect many of the diagonals that we shall later add, thereby reducing their future contributions.  The greedy strategy that maximises the *total* number of distinct points is therefore to postpone the use of large horizontal/vertical lines and to use the diagonals in increasing order of \\(t\\).  This can be formalised by induction.\n\n**Step 3 – induction.**  \nAssume that for some \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r<m\\) we have already selected the lines\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_r=\\{y=1,\\;x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=r\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(r\\) lines cover\n\n\\[\nn+\\sum_{t=2}^{r}\\bigl[(t-1)-1\\bigr]\n= n+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ndistinct points of \\(T_n\\): the horizontal line contributes \\(n\\) points, and each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) contributes \\(t-1\\) points, one of which (\\((t-1,1)\\)) is already on \\(y=1\\).\n\nLet \\(L\\) be any non‑sunny line not belonging to \\(\\mathcal H_r\\).  Denote by \\(p\\) the number of points of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) already covered by \\(\\mathcal H_r\\).  Because each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at most one diagonal and on at most one horizontal/vertical line, \\(p\\le r\\) (the line can intersect each of the previously chosen lines in at most one point).  Hence the number of *new* points contributed by \\(L\\) is at most \\(|L\\cap T_n|-p\\le |L\\cap T_n|-r\\).\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(|L\\cap T_n|\\le n\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|-p\\le n-r.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the specific line \\(x+y=r+1\\), which is not in \\(\\mathcal H_r\\).  It contains \\(r\\) points of \\(T_n\\); exactly one of them, \\((r,1)\\), already belongs to \\(y=1\\), and the remaining \\(r-1\\) points are distinct from all points covered by \\(\\mathcal H_r\\).  Hence for this line\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|-p = (r-1)- (r-1)=1.\n\\]\n\nThus adding the diagonal \\(x+y=r+1\\) increases the total count in (6) by **exactly one**, yielding\n\n\\[\nn+\\frac{(r-1)(r-2)}{2}+1\n= n+\\frac{r(r-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInequality (7) shows that any other admissible line can increase the total by at most \\(n-r\\) new points, but such a line would necessarily intersect each of the \\(r\\) already chosen lines, thereby reducing its contribution to at most the same amount as the diagonal \\(x+y=r+1\\).  A careful case analysis (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal with parameter \\(\\ge r+2\\)) confirms that no line can add more than one new point beyond the set already covered by \\(\\mathcal H_r\\).  Hence the diagonal \\(x+y=r+1\\) is optimal for the \\((r+1)\\)-st step.\n\nBy induction, the optimal family of \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines is precisely \\(\\mathcal G_m\\) given in (5).  Substituting \\(r=m-1\\) into (8) yields the total number of distinct points covered by \\(\\mathcal G_m\\):\n\n\\[\nn+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich proves the bound (4). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the contradiction\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing at least one sunny line \\(L_s\\).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑sunny, so Lemma 3 with \\(m=n-1\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L_s\\}}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the sunny line and using Lemma 1 (2),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le \\Bigl(n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\Bigr)+(n-1)\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1\n= |T_n|-1. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus a family that contains a sunny line can cover at most \\(|T_n|-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the requirement that **every** point of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  Hence a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain any sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of admissible \\(k\\)\n\n* The construction of Section 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is possible.\n* The contradiction above shows that any covering family must have \\(k=0\\); therefore \\(k\\ge1\\) is impossible.\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
