nny line the size of its intersection with \\(T_n\\) is given by formula (3) (horizontal/vertical gives \\(n+1-c\\), diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) gives \\(t-1\\)).\n\n   *Lemma 3 (maximal coverage by \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines).*  \n   For any integer \\(m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n-1\\) and any collection of \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines,\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl|\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\bigl(L_i\\cap T_n\\bigr)\\Bigr|\n   \\le n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   The proof proceeds by double counting the incidences between the chosen lines and the points of \\(T_n\\). It shows that the extremal configuration is exactly \\(\\mathcal G_m\\) defined above, and that any other choice cannot exceed the bound.\n\n   Applying Lemma 1 and Lemma 3 with \\(m=n-1\\) yields that a family containing a sunny line can cover at most \\(|T_n|-1\\) points, contradicting the requirement that all points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\n4. **Conclusion** – a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a sunny line; therefore the only possible number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\bigl\\{\\,y=1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct and non‑sunny (the diagonals are parallel to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\); \\(y=1\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=1\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\n  diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of any sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains at least one sunny line.  Choose a\nsunny line \\(L_s\\in\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one diagonal of the form\n\\(x+y=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).  Indeed, for \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n\\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); the equality \\(a+b=n+1\\) corresponds to the boundary\n\\(t=n+1\\), which contains no point of \\(T_n\\) because \\(b\\ge1\\) forces\n\\(a\\le n\\).  Hence the admissible diagonals are precisely those with\n\\(2\\le t\\le n\\), and each point determines its own value of \\(t=a+b\\).\n\nA sunny line is, by definition, not parallel to any of those diagonals; thus\nit meets each diagonal in at most one point.  Consequently it can meet at most\none point from each diagonal, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n|L_s\\cap T_n|\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or a diagonal parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\).  From the definition of \\(T_n\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL:y=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)      &\\Longrightarrow |L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c,\\\\\nL:x=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)      &\\Longrightarrow |L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c,\\\\\nL:x+y=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n)    &\\Longrightarrow |L\\cap T_n|=t-1.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (maximal number of points covered by \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(1\\le m\\le n-1\\) and let \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) be distinct non‑sunny lines.\nSet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\bigl(L_i\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nWe claim  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each line \\(L_i\\) denote by \\(\\alpha_i\\) the number of points of\n\\(L_i\\cap T_n\\) that are **new**, i.e. that do not belong to any\n\\(L_j\\) with \\(j<i\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\alpha_i. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe bound each \\(\\alpha_i\\) from above.\n\nFix the first \\(i-1\\) lines.  Any point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one\nhorizontal line, exactly one vertical line, and exactly one diagonal\n\\(x+y=t\\) (with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).  Hence a new line \\(L_i\\) can intersect each\nprevious line in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently the number of\npoints of \\(L_i\\cap T_n\\) that are already covered is at most \\(i-1\\).  Using\n(3),\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\le |L_i\\cap T_n|-(i-1)\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn+1-c-(i-1) &\\text{if }L_i\\text{ is horizontal or vertical},\\\\[2pt]\nt-1-(i-1)   &\\text{if }L_i\\text{ is diagonal}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand sides are maximised by taking a horizontal line with the\nlargest possible intersection, namely \\(y=1\\) (for which \\(c=1\\) and\n\\(|L_i\\cap T_n|=n\\)).  After this line has been chosen, the remaining lines\ncannot be horizontal or vertical with the same large value, because any such\nline would intersect the already chosen horizontal line in at most one point,\nso the term \\(-(i-1)\\) in (6) would reduce the contribution to at most\n\\(n-(i-1)\\le n-1\\).  In contrast, a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with the smallest\navailable \\(t\\) adds exactly \\(t-1-(i-1)\\) new points, and for the smallest\npossible \\(t\\) this quantity equals \\(i-1\\) (see below).\n\nThus the optimal strategy is:\n\n* first take the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n* then, for each subsequent step \\(i\\ge2\\), take the diagonal\n  \\(x+y=i\\) (which is not parallel to any previously chosen line).\n\nWe verify that this strategy indeed yields the bound (4).\n\nFor the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_m=\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le m\\},\n\\]\n\nthe number of covered points is computed directly from (3).  \nThe line \\(y=1\\) contributes \\(n\\) points.  \nFor each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le m\\)) the set\n\\(L\\cap T_n\\) contains \\(t-1\\) points, one of which, namely \\((t-1,1)\\),\nalready lies on \\(y=1\\).  Hence each diagonal contributes \\(t-2\\) *new*\npoints.  Summing,\n\n\\[\n|\\,\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal G_m}(L\\cap T_n)\\,|\n= n+\\sum_{t=2}^{m}(t-2)\n= n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich shows that the right‑hand side of (4) is attainable.\n\nIt remains to prove that no other collection can exceed this number.\nFrom (6) we have for each \\(i\\ge2\\)\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-(i-1) &\\text{if }L_i\\text{ is horizontal/vertical},\\\\\nt-i   &\\text{if }L_i\\text{ is diagonal }x+y=t.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause the diagonals are indexed by their sum \\(t\\), the smallest possible\n\\(t\\) among the diagonals not yet used is at most \\(i\\).  Hence for any\ndiagonal we have \\(t-i\\le 0\\).  The only way to obtain a positive contribution\nis to use the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (which gives \\(\\alpha_1=n\\)) and then,\nfor each subsequent step, a diagonal with \\(t=i\\), which yields\n\\(\\alpha_i=1\\).  Any horizontal or vertical line chosen after the first step\nhas \\(\\alpha_i\\le n-(i-1)\\le 1\\) for \\(i\\ge n\\), but since \\(m\\le n-1\\) we have\n\\(i\\le n-1\\) and thus \\(\\alpha_i\\le 1\\) as well.  Consequently the total\nsum (5) cannot exceed\n\n\\[\nn+\\underbrace{1+1+\\dots+1}_{m-1\\text{ times}}\n= n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2},\n\\]\n\nthe same value obtained by \\(\\mathcal G_m\\).  This completes the proof of\nLemma 3. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the contradiction\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing at\nleast one sunny line \\(L_s\\).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑sunny, so\nLemma 3 with \\(m=n-1\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L_s\\}}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the sunny line and using Lemma 1 (2),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le \\Bigl(n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\Bigr)+(n-1)\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-1\n= |T_n|-1. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus a family that contains a sunny line can cover at most \\(|T_n|-1\\) points\nof \\(T_n\\), contradicting the requirement that **every** point of \\(T_n\\) be\ncovered.  Hence a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain any\nsunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of admissible \\(k\\)\n\n* The construction of Section 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n* The contradiction above shows that any covering family must have \\(k=0\\); for\n  \\(k\\ge1\\) the covering condition cannot be satisfied.\n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny\nlines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
