ne the size of its intersection with \\(T_n\\) is given by formula (3) (horizontal/vertical gives \\(n+1-c\\), diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) gives \\(t-1\\)).\n\n   *Lemma 3 (maximal coverage by \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines).*  \n   For any integer \\(m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n-1\\) and any collection of \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines,\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl|\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\bigl(L_i\\cap T_n\\bigr)\\Bigr|\n   \\le n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   The proof proceeds by double counting the incidences between the chosen lines and the points of \\(T_n\\). It shows that the extremal configuration is exactly \\(\\mathcal G_m\\) defined above, and that any other choice cannot exceed the bound.\n\n   Applying Lemma 1 and Lemma 3 with \\(m=n-1\\) yields that a family containing a sunny line can cover at most \\(|T_n|-1\\) points, contradicting the requirement that all points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\n4. **Conclusion** – a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a sunny line; therefore the only possible number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\bigl\\{\\,y=1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct and non‑sunny (the diagonals are parallel to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\); \\(y=1\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=1\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n