vered by \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(1\\le m\\le n-1\\) and let \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) be distinct non‑sunny lines.\nSet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\bigl(L_i\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nWe claim  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each line \\(L_i\\) denote by \\(\\alpha_i\\) the number of points of\n\\(L_i\\cap T_n\\) that are **new**, i.e. that do not belong to any\n\\(L_j\\) with \\(j<i\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\alpha_i. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe bound each \\(\\alpha_i\\) from above.\n\nFix the first \\(i-1\\) lines.  Any point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one\nhorizontal line, exactly one vertical line, and exactly one diagonal\n\\(x+y=t\\) (with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).  Hence a new line \\(L_i\\) can intersect each\nprevious line in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently the number of\npoints of \\(L_i\\cap T_n\\) that are already covered is at most \\(i-1\\).  Using\n(3),\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\le |L_i\\cap T_n|-(i-1)\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn+1-c-(i-1) &\\text{if }L_i\\text{ is horizontal or vertical},\\\\[2pt]\nt-1-(i-1)   &\\text{if }L_i\\text{ is diagonal}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand sides are maximised by taking a horizontal line with the\nlargest possible intersection, namely \\(y=1\\) (for which \\(c=1\\) and\n\\(|L_i\\cap T_n|=n\\)).  After this line has been chosen, the remaining lines\ncannot be horizontal or vertical with the same large value, because any such\nline would intersect the already chosen horizontal line in at most one point,\nso the term \\(-(i-1)\\) in (6) would reduce the contribution to at most\n\\(n-(i-1)\\le n-1\\).  In contrast, a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with the smallest\navailable \\(t\\) adds exactly \\(t-1-(i-1)\\) new points, and for the smallest\npossible \\(t\\) this quantity equals \\(i-1\\) (see below).\n\nThus the optimal strategy is:\n\n* first take the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n* then, for each subsequent step \\(i\\ge2\\), take the diagonal\n  \