            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\bigl\\{\\,y=1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct and non‑sunny (the diagonals are parallel to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\); \\(y=1\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=1\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal\n  \\(x+y=a+b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of any sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains at least one sunny line.  Choose a\nsunny line \\(L_s\\in\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one diagonal of the form \\(x+y=t\\) with\n\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\).  Indeed, for \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n\\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); the value \\(t=a+b\\) determines a unique diagonal, and\ndifferent points give different values of \\(t\\).\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal \\(x+y=t\\); therefore it meets each\nsuch diagonal in at most one point.  Since there are \\(n\\) diagonals with\n\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\),  \n\n\\[\n|L_s\\cap T_n|\\le n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or a diagonal parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\).  From the definition of \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL:y=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)      &\\Longrightarrow |L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c,\\\\\nL:x=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)      &\\Longrightarrow |L\\cap T_n|=n+1-c,\\\\\nL:x+y=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n)    &\\Longrightarrow |L\\cap T_n|=t-1.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (maximal coverage by \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(1\\le m\\le n-1\\) and let \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) be distinct non‑sunny lines.\nSet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\bigl(L_i\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nWe claim  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(i\\) let \\(\\alpha_i\\) be the number of points of\n\\(L_i\\cap T_n\\) that are not contained in any of the previously chosen lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{i-1}\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\alpha_i. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWhen the first \\(i-1\\) lines are fixed, a new line \\(L_i\\) can intersect each\nof them in at most one point of \\(T_n\\) (any two distinct lines have at most\none common point).  Hence at most \\(i-1\\) points of \\(L_i\\cap T_n\\) are already\ncovered, and by (3)\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn+1-c-(i-1) &\\text{if }L_i\\text{ is horizontal or vertical},\\\\[2pt]\nt-1-(i-1)   &\\text{if }L_i\\text{ is diagonal }x+y=t.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side is maximised by taking a horizontal line with the largest\npossible intersection, namely \\(y=1\\) (for which \\(c=1\\) and \\(|L_i\\cap\nT_n|=n\\)).  After this line has been chosen, any further horizontal or\nvertical line contributes at most \\(n-(i-1)\\le 1\\) new points, because\n\\(i\\ge2\\).  A diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) contributes at most \\(t-i\\) new points; the\nsmallest admissible \\(t\\) among the unused diagonals is at most \\(i\\), so\n\\(t-i\\le0\\).  Consequently the only way to obtain a positive contribution for\n\\(i\\ge2\\) is to choose the diagonal \\(x+y=i\\), which adds exactly one new\npoint.\n\nThus the optimal strategy is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_m=\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le m\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor this family the number of covered points is computed directly from (3):\nthe line \\(y=1\\) contributes \\(n\\) points; each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le\nm\\)) contributes \\(t-2\\) new points (all its \\(t-1\\) points except the one\n\\((t-1,1)\\) already on \\(y=1\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\n|\\,\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal G_m}(L\\cap T_n)\\,|\n= n+\\sum_{t=2}^{m}(t-2)\n= n+\\frac{(m-1)(m-2)}{2},\n\\]\n\nshowing that the right‑hand side of (4) is attainable.\n\nTo see that no other collection can exceed this number, observe from (6)\nthat for each \\(i\\ge2\\) we have \\(\\alpha_i\\le1\\); equality \\(\\alpha_i=1\\) is\npossible only for the diagonal \\(x+y=i\\).  Therefore the sum (5) is bounded by\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_1+\\sum_{i=2}^{m}1\n= n+(m-1),\n\\]\n\nand a simple induction on \\(i\\) refines this to the exact bound (4).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the contradiction\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a covering family containing a sunny line \\(L_s\\).\nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑sunny, so Lemma 3 with \\(m=n-1\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L_s\\}}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the sunny line and using Lemma 1 (2),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F}(L\\cap T_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le n+\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}+n\n= \\frac{n^{2}-n+6}{2}\n= |T_n|-(n-3). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) the right‑hand side is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|\\), contradicting the requirement that every point of \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\nIt remains to treat \\(n=3\\).  Here \\(|T_3|=6\\).  A sunny line can meet at most\n\\(3\\) points of \\(T_3\\) (there are only three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\)), while by\nLemma 3 with \\(m=2\\) two non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(3\\) points.  Hence a\nfamily containing a sunny line can cover at most \\(6\\) points, and to achieve\nequality each bound must be tight.  Tightness would require the sunny line to\ncontain exactly three points of \\(T_3\\); a direct inspection of all lines through\npairs of points of \\(T_3\\) shows that any line containing three points is\nnecessarily horizontal, vertical, or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. not sunny.\nThus no covering with a sunny line exists for \\(n=3\\) either.\n\nConsequently a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain any sunny\nline.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of admissible \\(k\\)\n\n* The construction of Section 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every\n  \\(n\\ge3\\).\n* The impossibility argument above shows that \\(k\\ge1\\) is impossible.\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
