            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (extremal constructions)\n\n*Lemma 1.1.*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and together cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\). ∎\n\n*Lemma 1.2.*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{n}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{t}{n}(n+1)\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThen the \\(n\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are distinct, sunny, and cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slopes \\(\\frac{t}{n}\\) are never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\(t\\neq s\\) then the slopes differ, so \\(L_t\\neq L_s\\).  \n\nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{a-(n+1)} = \\frac{t}{n}\n\\]\n\nfor a unique integer \\(t\\) in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); solving for \\(t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nt = n\\frac{b-1}{a-(n+1)}\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this \\(t\\) into the equation of \\(L_t\\) yields exactly \\(b\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (reduction)\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k)\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{k}\\,(x-(n-k)) + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{t}{k}(k+1)\\Bigr)\\qquad(t=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\n(the latter family is empty when \\(k=0\\)).  \n\nThen the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_k=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and keep \\(b'=b\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b' = a-(n-k)+b \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a',b')\\in S_{k}'\\) where  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}'=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1,\\;x+y\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nApplying Lemma 1.2 to the triangle \\(S_{k}'\\) (with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\)) we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{k}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{t}{k}(k+1)\\Bigr),\\qquad t=1,\\dots ,k,\n\\]\n\nwhich cover \\(S_{k}'\\).  Translating each \\(\\ell_t\\) by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) yields exactly the lines \\(L_t\\) defined above; translation does not affect the slope, so the \\(L_t\\) remain sunny and distinct.  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) we have \\((a',b')\\in S_{k}'\\); consequently there exists a unique \\(t\\) with  \n\n\\[\nb' = \\frac{t}{k}\\,a' + 1-\\frac{t}{k}(k+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{t}{k}\\,(a-(n-k)) + 1-\\frac{t}{k}(k+1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) is covered by one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFor a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) the family \\(\\mathcal{F}_k