\}\\); the lines are distinct and, as shown below, together they cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Key Lemma (covering by lines of distinct slopes).**  \n   For any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the \\(m\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n     \\ell_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{m}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{t}{m}(m+1)\\Bigr),\\qquad t=1,\\dots ,m,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   are sunny, distinct and each point \\((x,y)\\) of the triangle  \n\n   \\[\n     T_m=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1,\\;x+y\\le m+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   lies on exactly one of the \\(\\ell_t\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   For a given \\((x,y)\\in T_m\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n     t = \\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{m\\,y}{x}\\bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le y\\le m+1-x\\) and \\(x\\ge 1\\), one checks that \\(1\\le t\\le m\\).  \n   Substituting \\(t\\) from (2) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n     y = \\frac{t}{m}x + 1-\\frac{t}{m}(m+1),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_t\\).  \n   Uniqueness of \\(t\\) follows from the strict monotonicity of the right‑hand side of (1) as a function of \\(t\\). ∎\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n\n   - **Non‑sunny part.**  \n     Use the vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n       V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     They are non‑sunny and cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n   - **Remaining points.**  \n     Define  \n\n     \\[\n       S'_k=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     The set of points of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered by the lines (3) is precisely  \n\n     \\[\n       T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\n          =\\{(a'+n-k,b')\\mid (a',b')\\in S'_k\\}.\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Sunny part.**  \n     Apply the Key Lemma with \\(m=k\\) to the triangle \\(S'_k\\).  \n     For each \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let  \n\n     \\[\n       \\ell_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{k}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{t}{k}(k+1)\\Bigr)\n     \\]\n\n     and translate it by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).  The translated lines are  \n\n     \\[\n       L_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{k}\\,(x-(n-k)) + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{t}{k}(k+1)\\Bigr),\n       \\qquad t=1,\\dots ,k.\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n     Translation does not change the slope, so each \\(L_t\\) is sunny; the slopes \\(\\frac{t}{k}\\) are distinct, hence the lines are distinct.\n\n   - **Verification of coverage.**  \n     Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Write \\((a',b')=(a-(n-k),b)\\); then \\((a',b')\\in S'_k\\).  \n     By the Key Lemma there exists a unique \\(t\\) with  \n\n     \\[\n       b' = \\frac{t}{k}\\,a' + 1-\\frac{t}{k}(k+1).\n     \\]\n\n     Substituting back \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\) yields exactly the equation of \\(L_t\\) in (5); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).  \n     Consequently every point of \\(T_k\\) is covered by one of the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   - **Putting everything together.**  \n     The family  \n\n     \\[\n       \\mathcal{F}_k = \\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n     \\]\n\n     consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and by the previous paragraphs it covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   By definition the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, so \\(k>n\\) is impossible.  \n   The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \n\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
