\[\n\\{(a,1),\\;(a+1,2),\\dots ,(n+1-b,b)\\}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(b\\) ranging from \\(1\\) up to \\(n+1-a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Solving (4) for integer \\(b\\) gives \\(b = a + (b-1) - a + 1 = b\\); thus for each integer \\(b\\ge 1\\) the point \\((a+b-1,b)\\) satisfies (4).  The condition \\(a+b-1\\le n\\) is equivalent to \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), which is exactly the restriction defining \\(S_n\\).  Hence the listed points are precisely the intersection of \\(L_a\\) with \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k)\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nL_a:\\; y = x-a+1\\qquad(a=n-k+1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThe \\(V_i\\) are vertical, thus non‑sunny; the \\(L_a\\) have slope \\(1\\), thus sunny.  All lines are distinct because the vertical lines have undefined slope while the \\(L_a\\) have slope \\(1\\), and among the \\(L_a\\) the intercept \\(-a+1\\) differs for different \\(a\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) by Lemma 1.\n* If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) then, by Lemma 2, \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_k=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### Exhaustion of possible \\(k\\)\n\nSince a sunny line is a line, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines; therefore \\(k\\le n\\).  The construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
