(\\mathcal F_0\\) is the set of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non\u2011sunny).  \n   *\\(k=n\\): \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) is the set of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) (no vertical line is used).\n\n6. **Impossibility of other values.**  \n   Since a sunny line is a line, we must have \\(k\\le n\\).  The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Hence the admissible values are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), not \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and not \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Vertical lines cover the leftmost columns)\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set of vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,m)\n\\]\n\ncovers exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  A point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_i\\) iff \\(a=i\\).  Hence the claim follows directly from the definition of the lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny lines of slope\u202f1 cover the upper rows)\n\nFix an integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and set  \n\n\\[\na_b:=n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_b:\\; y = x - (a_b-b)=x-(n+1-2b).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(L_b\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny) and\n\n\\[\nL_b\\cap S_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a_b\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Substituting \\(y=b\\) into (3) gives \\(b = a-(a_b-b)\\), i.e. \\(a=a_b\\).  Thus the point \\((a_b,b)=(n+1-b,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_b\\).  \n\nFor any integer \\(t\\ge 0\\) with \\(a_b+t\\le n+1-b\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = (a_b+t) - (a_b-b),\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((a_b+t,b)\\) also satisfies (3).  The condition \\(a_b+t\\le n+1-b\\) is equivalent to \\(t\\le (n+1-b)-a_b = b-1\\), which is precisely the range of admissible first coordinates for points of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\).  Consequently every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge a_b\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-b\\) lies on \\(L_b\\), and no other point of \\(S_n\\) lies on \\(L_b\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3 (The family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers all of \\(S_n\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and define the family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) by (2).  \nThen every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Take an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b\\le k\\) and consequently \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) (because \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(k\\ge n-k+1\\) only when \\(k=n\\); in any case the inequality \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) holds for the points not covered by the vertical lines).  \nHence \\(b\\) is one of the indices of the sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\), namely \\(b\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_b\\), which is a member of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nIn both cases the point is covered, proving the lemma. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction\n\nFor a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  The set contains exactly \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines.  \nThe first \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical (non\u2011sunny); the remaining \\(k\\) lines are of slope \\(1\\) and therefore sunny.  The intercepts in (3) are \\(2b-n-1\\) for distinct \\(b\\), so the sunny lines are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the vertical lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  By Lemma\u202f3 every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, satisfying the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Extremal cases\n\n* \\(k=0\\): \\(\\mathcal F_0=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) (all vertical, none sunny).  \n* \\(k=n\\): \\(\\mathcal F_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) (all sunny, no vertical line).\n\nBoth are specialisations of the general construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nA sunny line is a line, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Hence no other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

