ach point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the original lines, and we have kept a line through each of those points.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of any of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v_1=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf v_2=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf v_3=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nHence the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the *non\u2011sunny* directions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering with no sunny lines ( \\(k=0\\) )\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: x=i \\qquad (1\\le i\\le n-1),\\\\\nM   &: y=1,\\\\\nD   &: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (1) belong to \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\); therefore they are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Covering proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in M\\).  \nIf \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) because  \n\n\\[\na = (a+b)-b \\le (n+1)-2 = n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\) and \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).  \nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of (1).  \n\nConsequently a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Replacing non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones\n\nFix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(1\\le s\\le n\\).  \nWe shall construct a configuration containing exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\nSelect \\(s\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\) as follows.  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,s\\}\\) choose the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)\\in T_n .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(P_i\\) lies on the vertical line 