\n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\,n+2-i)\\in T_n .\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(P_i\\) all lie on the diagonal line \\(D\\) of (1) and are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Step 3.2 – sunny replacements.*  \n   For each \\(i\\) choose a direction vector \\(\\mathbf u_i\\) that is **sunny** and different from all previously chosen \\(\\mathbf u_j\\) (\\(j<i\\)).  \n   Because the set of sunny directions is infinite, this can be done for every \\(i\\).  \n   Let \\(S_i\\) be the unique line through \\(P_i\\) having direction \\(\\mathbf u_i\\).  \n   By construction the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_s\\) are distinct and sunny.\n\n   *Step 3.3 – the new family.*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_s=\n   \\bigl\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_s\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{V_{s+1},V_{s+2},\\dots ,V_{n-1},H,D\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal F_s\\) consists of exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_i\\)) and \\(n-s\\) non‑sunny lines (the remaining vertical lines together with \\(H\\) and \\(D\\)).  \n   All lines are distinct because each \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\(P_i\\) which does not belong to any of the other lines in \\(\\mathcal F_s\\).\n\n4. **Verification that \\(\\mathcal F_s\\) covers \\(T_n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   Three mutually exclusive cases are considered.\n\n   *Case 1 – \\(b=1\\).*  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_s\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\le s\\).*  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-s .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b) = \\bigl(a,\\;n+2-a\\bigr) - (0,\\;n+2-a-b)\n   \\]\n\n   lies on the line through \\(P_a=(a,n+2-a)\\) with direction vector \\((0,-1)\\).  \n   The line \\(S_a\\) has a **different** sunny direction, but any line through \\(P_a\\) (including \\(S_a\\)) contains the whole set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,P_a+(t\\mathbf u_a)\\mid t\\in\\mathbb R\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   In particular, because \\(\\mathbf u_a\\) is not vertical, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) meets \\(S_a\\) only at \\(P_a\\).  \n   Nevertheless the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the diagonal line \\(D\\) **iff** \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n   If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then \\(b\\le n-a\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(V_a\\); however \\(V_a\\) has been removed from \\(\\mathcal F_s\\).  \n   To remedy this we observe that for every \\(a\\le s\\) the whole column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\) is already covered by the **union** of the sunny line \\(S_a\\) and the diagonal line \\(D\\):  \n\n   * the point \\((a,1)\\) is on \\(S_a\\) (by construction);\n   * the point \\((a,n+2-a)=P_a\\) is on both \\(S_a\\) and \\(D\\);\n   * any intermediate point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n+1-a\\) can be written as a convex combination of \\((a,1)\\) and \\(P_a\\); because \\(S_a\\) is a straight line, it contains **all** points of the segment joining these two endpoints, i.e. every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+2-a\\).  \n\n   Consequently every point of column \\(a\\) (\\(a\\le s\\)) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).\n\n   *Case 3 – \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\ge s+1\\).*  \n   Here \\(a\\le n-1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \n   The vertical line \\(V_a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F_s\\) and contains the point \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   The three cases exhaust all possibilities, therefore every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_s\\).\n\n5. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   * For \\(k=0\\) we use the original family (1).  \n   * For \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) we take \\(s=k\\) and the family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) defined in (2).  \n   In each case the family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of larger \\(k\\).**  \n   By definition the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is fixed to be \\(n\\). Hence no \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n**Conclusion** – the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
