 *Diagonal line.*  \n   The points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\) all satisfy \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   By the same argument, a line that is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain at most one of these points.  \n   Therefore the diagonal line \\(D\\) must also belong to every admissible family.\n\n4. **All remaining lines must be vertical.**  \n\n   After fixing \\(H\\) and \\(D\\), the points that are still uncovered are those with  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\ge 2,\\qquad a+b\\le n,\\qquad\\text{hence }1\\le a\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed column \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n-2\\)) the set of uncovered points in that column is  \n\n   \\[\n   C_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-a\\},\n   \\qquad |C_a|=n-a-1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Claim.*  To cover all points of \\(C_a\\) we need at least \\(|C_a|\\) distinct lines that intersect the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Any line that is **not** vertical meets the line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point; consequently it can contribute at most one element of \\(C_a\\).  Hence each non\u2011vertical line can cover at most one point of \\(C_a\\).  To cover \\(|C_a|\\) points we therefore need at least \\(|C_a|\\) distinct lines intersecting \\(x=a\\). \u220e\n\n   Summing the lower bounds (3) over all columns gives the total number of required line\u2011column intersections:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{a=1}^{n-2} |C_a|\n   =\\sum_{a=1}^{n-2} (n-a-1)\n   =\\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} k\n   =\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   After the mandatory lines \\(H\\) and \\(D\\) we have exactly \\(n-2\\) lines left.  \n   If any of these remaining lines were not vertical, it would intersect each column \\(x=a\\) in at most one point, contributing at most one to the sum (4) for **each** column.  Hence the total number of line\u2011column intersections contributed by the \\(n-2\\) remaining lines would be at most \\((n-2)(n-2)\\).\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 4\\),\n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)(n-2)<\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}