-------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_c\\) have slope \\(-1\\), hence are non‑sunny, and they are distinct.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_c\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n^{(0)}=\\{L_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction that can increase the number of sunny lines by one\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) we have a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_m=\\{\\,\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(m\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n* \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}\\ell_i\\supseteq S_m\\);\n* exactly \\(t\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny, where \\(0\\le t\\le m\\).\n\nWe shall produce a family \\(\\mathcal F_{m+1}\\) of \\(m+1\\) lines covering \\(S_{m+1}\\) and containing either \\(t\\) or \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1 Adding a new non‑sunny line\n\nConsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nL_{new}: x+y=m+2 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-1\\), so it is non‑sunny and it is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal F_m\\) (all those lines belong to \\(\\mathcal F_m\\) and have slopes different from \\(-1\\) or are already among the diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\le m+1\\)).  \n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G:=\\mathcal F_m\\cup\\{L_{new}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(m+1\\) distinct lines and clearly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal G}\\ell = S_m\\cup\\{(a,m+2-a)\\mid a=1,\\dots ,m+1\\}=S_{m+1}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal G\\) is a valid configuration for \\(m+1\\) with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2 Replacing the new diagonal by a sunny line (optional)\n\nSelect any point \\((a_0,b_0)\\in S_m\\) that lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F_m\\); such a point exists because \\(\\mathcal F_m\\) covers \\(S_m\\).  \nChoose a point \\((a_1,b_1)\\) on the newly added diagonal \\(L_{new}\\); for definiteness take \\((a_1,b_1)=(1,m+1)\\).\n\nDefine \\(M\\) to be the unique line passing through \\((a_0,b_0)\\) and \\((a_1,b_1)\\). Its slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_1-b_0}{a_1-a_0}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a_0,b_0)\\notin L_{new}\\), the numerator and denominator are not equal in magnitude with opposite sign, and the denominator is non‑zero; consequently the slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). Hence \\(M\\) is sunny.\n\nNow replace \\(L_{new}\\) by \\(M\\) and set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{m+1}:=\\mathcal F_m\\cup\\{M\\}.\n\\]\n\n*All points of \\(S_m\\) remain covered*: each such point was already on some line of \\(\\mathcal F_m\\); the replacement does not remove any of those lines.  \n*The point \\((a_1,b_1)\\) is covered*: it lies on the newly inserted sunny line \\(M\\).  \n*All other points of the diagonal \\(L_{new}\\) are already covered*: they belong to \\(S_m\\) (the diagonal \\(x+y=m+2\\) meets the triangle only at its single endpoint \\((a_1,b_1)\\)), so they are covered by \\(\\mathcal F_m\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_{m+1}\\) still covers the whole set \\(S_{m+1}\\) and now contains \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently, from a configuration for \\(m\\) we can obtain a configuration for \\(m+1\\) with either the same number of sunny lines or one more.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nStart with the base configuration \\(\\mathcal F_3^{(0)}\\) (the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\)), which has \\(k=0\\).  \nApply the inductive step repeatedly:\n\n* After the first application we obtain a configuration for \\(4\\) lines with either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) sunny line.\n* After the second application we obtain a configuration for \\(5\\) lines with any number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(2\\), and so on.\n\nBy performing the “replace‑by‑sunny‑line’’ part of the step exactly \\(k\\) times (and the “add‑non‑sunny’’ part the remaining \\(n-3\\) times) we arrive at a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(S_n\\);\n* contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the step can be executed as long as we have not yet reached \\(k=n\\), every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Upper bound\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence no value \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
