            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(d_c\\) have slope \\(-1\\), hence are non‑sunny, and they are distinct because the constants differ.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(d_c\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n:=\\{d_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that pass through the “top diagonal’’ points\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) with maximal sum of coordinates are  \n\n\\[\nP_a=(a,n+1-a),\\qquad a=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(a\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_a:\\; y=\\frac{n+1-a}{a}\\,x .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_a\\) passes through \\((0,0)\\) and \\(P_a\\).  \nIts slope \\(\\frac{n+1-a}{a}\\) is a positive rational number; it is never \\(0\\) (numerator \\(\\neq0\\)), never \\(\\infty\\) (denominator \\(\\neq0\\)), and never \\(-1\\) because both numerator and denominator are positive. Hence every \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\(a\\neq a'\\) then the slopes \\(\\frac{n+1-a}{a}\\) and \\(\\frac{n+1-a'}{a'}\\) differ, so the lines \\(\\ell_a\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose any subset \\(A=\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_k\\}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of size \\(k\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k\n   =\\bigl\\{\\ell_{a_i}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\bigr\\}\n    \\cup\\bigl\\{\\,d_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n+1-a_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\}\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe first part contributes exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; the second part contributes the remaining \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines, all of which are non‑sunny. Hence \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers \\(S_n\\)\n\n*Case 1: points with \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \nFor such a point set \\(c:=a+b\\). Since \\(2\\le c\\le n\\), the diagonal line \\(d_c\\) belongs to the second part of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); therefore the point lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Case 2: points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \nThese are exactly the points \\(P_a\\) from (2.1).  \n\n- If \\(a\\in A\\), then \\(P_a\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\) which is in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).  \n- If \\(a\\notin A\\), then the constant \\(c=n+1\\) is **not** removed from the second part of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) (because we removed only those constants of the form \\(n+1-a_i\\) with \\(a_i\\in A\\)). Hence the diagonal line \\(d_{\\,n+1}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) and contains \\(P_a\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nWe must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Consequently no integer \\(k>n\\) can satisfy the requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe construction above shows that each such \\(k\\) can be realized, and the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
